{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons","id":1360544,"title":"Homebrew & Headcanons","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-01-12T10:11:43-08:00","id":1360544,"title":"Homebrew & Headcanons","content":"Heya y'all!<br /> <br /> This is a thread for people's homebrew and head canons and setting thoughts and alternate takes on the WoD and all that other goodness that keeps us nerds going.<br /> <br /> I always like threads like these and take a lot of inspiration from them and I'm genuinely interested in what thoughts y'all have, so thank ya for reading this and I hope you fellow Mage fans participate or at least have fun reading this.<br /> <br /> Anyway, without further ado, let me just say I'm excited to see what this has in store!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-13T07:19:49-08:00","id":1360688,"title":"","content":"I thought this was how it worked, but apparently a Houserule.<br /> <br /> When conducting ritual magic against a target with inherent countermagick, you roll the first ritual Arete Roll, then Countermagick against that first roll. Which can cause it to fail as normal. If the Countermagick doesn't cause the ritual to fail, continue on with the ritual as normal.<br /> <br /> Secondly, For the most part it's my head Canon that the Technocracy and Traditions simply don't have all that many low Arete members and are more or less the boogeymen in the background of the setting.<br /> <br /> Most mages are Orphans, Or sometimes members of what I called Orphanages which are basically groups of Orphans with the same or a similar paradigm. Kind of a Mini Craft.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11744,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","user":"Enginseer-42","date":". 01-13-2020, 10:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-13T14:31:15-08:00","id":1360761,"title":"","content":"That would definitely be an interesting framing device for a Mage game, particularly a street level goth-punk one, playing as Orphans with the Traditions &amp; Technocrats both as monolithic and mysterious structures.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-13T15:50:53-08:00","id":1360777,"title":"","content":"Too many to mention, as Mage was one of the first games I started taking apart and rebuilding after Time of Judgment. Unfortunately, I lost the entire post I spent a good half hour writing, and don't feel like trying to rewrite it right now. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I will mention that when doing Mage: The Dark Ages (as opposed to DA:M), I use the usual modern day magic system and my own Fellowships. (The Subtle Ones, The Gardeners of the Tree of Life, the Craftmasons, the Memento Mori, The Keepers of the Sacred Fire, The Nine Sacred Passions, and The Voices of Mercy), with the Pax Hermetica and the Society of Pure Thought being their own stand alone things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 01-13-2020, 06:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-13T16:12:54-08:00","id":1360781,"title":"","content":"I feel that No One of Consequence, nothing quite like losing all the wind from your sails because a forum error destroyed your works.<br /> <br /> That does sound quite interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-13T17:32:05-08:00","id":1360792,"title":"","content":"I'll probably just post drips and drabs. Such as that, as I've mentioned in the other threads, I use the CoD/Storypath Attribute set up, with its Physical/Social/Mental and Power/Finesse/Resilience axi. (I cap them at 5 for all my games, but have guidelines for Legendary Attributes, which operate a little like Scion's Scale. You don't see it in Mage so much outside of certain Bygones and Constructs, but it's more common with my elder vampires, legendary werebeasts and the like.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-14T15:45:58-08:00","id":1360972,"title":"","content":"As I've said in other threads, I use a very slimmed down list of Abilities, with eight in each category.<br /> <br /> Talents: Animal Ken, Athletics, Awareness, Enigmas, Melee<sup>1</sup>, Presence, Subterfuge<br /> Skills: Crafts, Drive, Firearms, Larceny, Performance, Socialize, Survival, Weaponry<br /> Knowledge: Academics, Bureaucracy<sup>2</sup>, Investigation, Lore<sup>3</sup>, Medicine, Occult<sup>3</sup>, Science, Technology<br /> <br /> I prefer fewer skills because of the issue of &quot;skill creep&quot;. This is a phenomenon that was, to my knowledge, first noticed with the GURPS system. Likely because so many books were written by people who were science and tech people, it was fairly common for every sourcebook to introduce newer and more specialized versions of existing skills. The problem with this is that the more skills there are, the more you have to spread out your skill points, and this tends to result in less competent starting characters. Conversely, the fewer skills, the further your starting points go.<br /> Ironically, I soon found that with only 24 Abilities, I was able to cut back on the number of starting Ability points to 11/7/4. (Part of this is also because I made Arete 2 the default starting level for all characters, meaning no one had to spend those four bonus points, and so had more to spend on other things.)<br /> <br /> During character creation, I specifically ask people to figure out just what each skill represents to the character. If you have Medicine, is it EMT training? Are you a surgeon? Or is it tradition cultural healing methods like herbalism, acupuncture, and the like? Fringe medical theories from the Victorian age? Or several of the above? <br /> <br /> Additionally, I started using 1st ed Exalted's Specialty system, with everyone getting 3 Specialty dots/points to add to their Abilities at character creation, giving extra dice to role in situations that involved said specialty. I found this especially useful for things like Crafts, Academics and the like, where one could have Academics 1 (Law +2), Crafts 1 (Carpentry +3), or Medicine 1 (Herbalism +1).<br /> <br /> <sup>1</sup>By Melee I mean hand to hand combat, as that is closer to the definition of the word.<br /> <sup>2</sup>Bureaucracy in this case isn't just how well someone can navigate the modern world of forms, regulations, and agencies, but also one's own skill a organization and management.<br /> <sup>3</sup>Lore became the skill that represents how much a character knows about his own supernatural subculture (For Mages, what they know about the Traditions, various rules and protocols, their own Tradition and its culture and history, various aspects of Awakened metaphysics, knowledge of their enemies, how not to make a muppet of yourself in front of Masters and Archmagi, and so forth.) Occult ended up being the measure of what one might know about other supernatural entities (including spirits and bygones).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 01-14-2020, 06:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-15T06:20:57-08:00","id":1361063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1360761#post1360761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That would definitely be an interesting framing device for a Mage game, particularly a street level goth-punk one, playing as Orphans with the Traditions &amp; Technocrats both as monolithic and mysterious structures.</div> </div> </div> </div>In essence yeah. I tend to play Mage one of two ways.<br /> <br /> 1.) Low level mage where I try to emulate the feel of the Pact serial. Very much try to make it so it's freeform, but focus on the learning. Focus on low level mages, most high power groups are old families who are mainly powerful by virtue of numbers rather than raw power. Arete 3 is a big deal etc etc.<br /> <br /> 2.) A fairly straightforward Tippyfying of the base setting, where the game basically becomes an empire building game where your goal is to expand the influence of YOUR reality zone while interfering with the opposing sides expansion. Very high power, but the goals are adjusted to match.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://pactwebserial.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://s0.wp.com/i/blank.jpg?m=1383295312i\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://pactwebserial.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\"> Pact </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://pactwebserial.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\"> https://pactwebserial.wordpress.com/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Devils and Details </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-15T14:23:32-08:00","id":1361163,"title":"","content":"The trimmed down ability system you have No One Of Consequence definitely sounds interesting and I'm particularly impressed for the reason that I know for a sure fire fact that my brain wouldn't be able to grok the mechanical issues at play without a lotta work.<br /> <br /> Also both of those styles sound very, very cool Engiseer and I always love reference to the TippyVerse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T11:46:51-08:00","id":1361533,"title":"","content":"<ul><li>The Technocracy is a totalitarian state Illuminati, and it defeated the Traditions. But it also lost the Ascension War. It lost the Ascension War because it is terminally corrupted and infiltrated by the Nephandi.</li> <li>The Traditions are a grab-bag of terrorist groups that finally failed in their objectives, i.e. the lost they Ascension War, because they failed to work together worth a damn. This is because each individual Tradition want to replace the totalitarian state of the Technocracy with their own totalitarian state, and they resented the mere existence of the other Traditions. As a group they are, however, largely free of Nephandi influence. But that is damning with faint praise.</li> <li>The Nephandi are winning the Ascension War and the situation has long past the point of no return.</li> </ul><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T13:02:26-08:00","id":1361547,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Grumpy RPG Reviews</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1361533#post1361533\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>The Technocracy is a totalitarian state Illuminati, and it defeated the Traditions. But it also lost the Ascension War. It lost the Ascension War because it is terminally corrupted and infiltrated by the Nephandi.</li> <li>The Traditions are a grab-bag of terrorist groups that finally failed in their objectives, i.e. the lost they Ascension War, because they failed to work together worth a damn. This is because each individual Tradition want to replace the totalitarian state of the Technocracy with their own totalitarian state, and they resented the mere existence of the other Traditions. As a group they are, however, largely free of Nephandi influence. But that is damning with faint praise.</li> <li>The Nephandi are winning the Ascension War and the situation has long past the point of no return.</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div>Still think that makes no sense. Why is the faction that's even less organized or willing to work with each other than The Traditions winning?<br /> <br /> They regularly kill each other off because of infighting, are split into their own warring sub-factions that hate each other on principle, are mercilessly hunted by the 2 most powerful organizations in their setting, have a meat grinder of a training program that means apprentices are unlikely to live long enough to see arete 3, and recruit their numbers exclusively from individuals with the rarest trait in setting(an awakening).<br /> <br /> The nephandi should honestly be as one step above being disparate as orphans or individual crafts. How in the qliphoth did they scrape together the resources, leadership, planning, and discipline needed to infiltrate the largest and most organized collection of the most powerful magic users with the most advanced science and magic the world has too offer... you know, unless they just use plot armor and authorial bias.<br /> <br /> Mage is the Odd duck in WOD where the themes of grimdark don't really make quite as much sense as other game lines. The entire scope of the game is about personal enlightment and absolute freedom, The originally establish antagonists(the Technocracy) are as good if not better than the games chosen &quot;Good guys&quot;, and the basic premise of the game (Ascension) was a vague and seemly unreachable concept, until you realize that failing to ascend before you die just means you'll have to start over when you reincarnate. The game in earlier editions even outright stated in earlier editions that the nephandic goal of &quot;Descension&quot; was likely impossible, simply do to the fact you can't Really destroy anything for good(it'll just be something else) and poking a hole in reality isn't so much like popping a balloon as it is like sticking a needle into the ocean and expecting it to pop.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T13:43:00-08:00","id":1361555,"title":"","content":"The Nephandi do not need a sophisticated ideology, or carefully coordination. They just need to poison make ideologically deadly, to riddle institutions with ontogolical cancer, to make people emotionally give up and either just go through the motions of life and faith, or to lash out with lethal violence in an effort to make themselves feel better. And the Nephandi are very good at all of that. It does not need to be a vast and careful plot, it just needs patience on the part of the Nephandi and a willingness to exploit the bitterness of members of the Technocracy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T13:49:13-08:00","id":1361559,"title":"","content":"While I respect Grumpy RPG's perspective, even if it is a bit farther in one direction for my taste, I can definitely see where he's coming from and I do think it's a solid setting model.<br /> <br /> To what you're saying Prometheas, I can also sympathize but I usually frame my setting further towards Grumpy's perspective.<br /> <br /> \u2022The reason why the Technocratic Union is winning to me is fairly obvious as barring the optional plot-thread of a Union civil war, the Union is the most organized and benefits from a centralized bureaucracy guiding its resources and efforts. The Enlightened Operatives of the Union are independently weaker on average when compared to Tradition Mages yet they benefit from vastly broader resources and support networks that outstrip the Traditions by a country mile and however disorganized the Union becomes, its internal infighting is still apart of an organization which is far more cohesive then either the Council of Traditions or Disparate Alliance.<br /> <br /> \u2022I can respect the Nephandi position but I feel that having the Nephandi be an active threat of corruption within both organizations is fitting when one considers the numerous issues of either. I personally dislike having Nephandi corruption being the sole cause but I agree that it aggravates the issues and mirroring the corruption of modernized values that we see irl, that corruption is a menace within the Union. It's own organization and coherence allows corruption to better entrench and to greater effect. I agree that by pure numbers true Nephandi are likely to br few, but their effect is broadly disproportionate to what one expect based on their numbers thanks to their tactics.<br /> <br /> \u2022Ultimately I think that when referring to the modern west the fact that it's in a state of Technocratic victory is undeniable. I wouldn't say that the victory is perfect or that the Traditions &amp; Crafts aren't waging a strong guerrilla campaign to destabilize the Union, but broadly speaking the modern western consensus is Technocratic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T14:46:41-08:00","id":1361575,"title":"","content":"In my World of Darkness, the Technocratic Focus is dominant; but the Technocracy itself is another matter: it's in the middle of a <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/992558-shard-the-technocratic-civil-war\">Civil War</a> after Control attempted a purge. The so-called Technephandi might be behind it, or the purge may have been initiated to root out the Technephandi, or the cause of the purge may have had nothing to do with the Nephandi; no one knows for sure. But whoever was behind the purge miscalculated, and now a new Order of Reason is fighting the Union.<br /> <br /> Another thing I've changed in my World of Darkness is that the Syndicate's Special Projects Department isn't Pentex; it's the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1009152-spd-a-new-take\">remnants of those Electrodyne Engineers who didn't abandon the Union when the Sons of Ether were formed</a>; the Syndicate sheltered them in exchange for having a secret group of outside-the-box thinkers on its payroll. They're now almost exclusively with the Order of Reason, as they're exactly the sort of Technocrats that would have been on the top of the Union's purge list; but they've <i>been</i> the bulk of the SPD all along. Ironically, the one part of the SPD that <i>didn't</i> get purged was the part that had gotten into bed with Pentex, which calls into doubt the claims that the purge was aimed at ridding the Union of Nephandic influences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-14-2023, 03:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T16:55:39-08:00","id":1361607,"title":"","content":"I made mention of my starting Arete at level 2 by default. However, at some point, I started calling it Gnosis instead. A large part of this is how I tend to approach the game. I look at it as a game of Dark Gnosticism; Those who have Awakened have gained knowledge of the secret underpinnings of reality, and with it the dangers of knowing too much. This secret and sacred knowledge can provide one with the keys to unimaginable power and ultimate enlightenment, but it also thrusts one into a world absent of the comfortable illusions and full of justifiable paranoia. <br /> (I use this as a generic term for Western Mages. Most Traditions have their own term for it, as do various cultures around the world.)<br /> I also stopped using the Avatar and Arcane backgrounds, tying them to one's Gnosis/Arete level. The more one's Gnosis grows, the more powerful and vocal their Avatar gets. But the down side is that one also gets more divorced from mundane reality. You gradually start to &quot;drop off the radar&quot;, so to speak, and eventually even mundane friends and family have trouble remembering you. This is even worse for Masters and especially Arch-mages, who can barely interact with mundane people and even have trouble with those of lesser Gnosis/Arete. <br /> Their method of getting around this is something I partially borrowed from Unknown Armies and its Invisible Clergy. Arch-mages can take on Masks based on various mystical/cultural archetypes (The Mage, The Emperor, The High Priestess, The Hermit, The Engineer, The Paladin, The Trickster, etc.), where its easier to interact with people and have them remember you when you conform to certain archetypical patterns and activities. I used Porthos Fitz-Empress in a lot of my games as a mentor type for younger mages, and have him using the Merlin mask, which makes it easier for him to continue to operate in the world as long as he's acting as a mentor and teacher and behind the scenes mastermind the way Merlin did for King Arthur. <br /> I started doing this because I keep things earth centric. Chantries are all on earth, though the use of various forms of magic allow them to be hidden in very interesting places. The Umbra/Otherworlds are full of wonders, knowledge, dangers, and ancient beings, but most Mages don't live there. But at the same time, I don't want Arch-mages overwhelming everything, so having it where they have a hard time actually interacting with others solves that problem for me, as they go off to be Exemplars, Oracles and eventually ascend.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-18T16:48:48-08:00","id":1361812,"title":"","content":"I have to say that I've heard the mechanical argument about Arete 2 for a long time and I've used that for a while but I really like what you've said here No One of Consequence about the idea of having the Arete/Gnosis attribute tie directly into such things as Avatar and/or Arcane and I think that makes a lot of sense as well as just being a very neat idea which I feel does a really good job of adding mystical flavor to mage development.<br /> <br /> Also, apropos of nothing, I'm curious about your nickname on the server as I always feel odd writing it out. Is it a reference to something?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-19T11:46:04-08:00","id":1361937,"title":"","content":"Regarding the Avatar, I've never really liked that name, as I don't think fits all that well (IMO at least), but I've also never been able to come up with a suitable replacement term for it. I've considered Daemon and Genius, among others, but none of them seem to fit what I'm looking for. But that aside:<br /> <br /> I treat the Avatar as an outside influence, a shard of semi-aware primal energy that bonds with a mage's soul (or psyche or whatever term you want to use) during the Awakening. There seems to be a limited number of them, and no one is born with them. They can carry over memories from one incarnation to another, so that some Awakened are gifted/cursed/annoyed with visions of &quot;past lives&quot;, but it's the Avatar's previous incarnation, not the person's. (Although cultures that believe in reincarnation believe that some Avatars seek out the same reincarnated soul over and over again.) It is also a popular theory that the Avatars are fragments of the beings that once oversaw the Tapestry and the functioning of reality itself in the period before creation was shattered into it's current state. Some think that each being may have fractured into four parts, one of each of the Essences, but there's no way to really know for sure. <br /> <br /> I also borrowed the idea of Auras for the four Essences. This is both a personal aura for each of the Awakened, as well as frequently adding a resonance to their magic. (I never really got into Revised Resonance system very much.) Archetypes also seem to be common among the four types. <br /> <br /> Dynamic - affiliated with the forces of creation and chaos, the element of fire, the gall humour, the season of Spring, the Eastern direction, the Sea, the suit of Wands, and the future.<br /> Aura: Energy and Drive<br /> Archetypes: The Advocate, The Fool, The Rebel, The Wanderer, The Warrior<br /> <br /> Pattern - affiliated with the forces of order and stasis, the element of earth, the phlegm humour, the season of Winter, the Western direction, the Land, the suit of Coins, and the present.<br /> Aura: Calm and Stability<br /> Archetypes: The Builder, The Defender, The Judge, The Teacher, The Watcher<br /> <br /> Primordial - affiliated with the forces of change and destruction, the element of water, the bile humour, the season of Autumn, the Southern direction, the Underworld, the suit of Swords, and the past.<br /> Aura: Eeriness and Loss<br /> Archetypes: The Destroyer, The Enforcer, The Hermit, The Madman, The Purifier<br /> <br /> Questing - affiliated with the forces of balance and vision, the element of air, the sanguine humour, the season of Summer, the Northern direction, the Heavens, the suit of Cups, and infinity.<br /> Aura: Focus and Dedication<br /> Archetypes: The Creator, The Crusader, The Emissary, The Healer, The Seeker<br /> <br /> <br /> (It's a <a href=\"https://youtu.be/nZIlAExvneo\" target=\"_blank\">Princess Bride</a> reference.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-19T13:19:07-08:00","id":1361967,"title":"","content":"One side effect of my games being earth centered, and also locally focused (at least to start with), is that there's a much stronger sense of local politics. <br /> Above the Cabal and Chantry level, I have Councils. These function as the leadership of a city or other geographical region's Awakened population (mainly that of Tradition members, but it may sometimes speak for others). A Council is made up of representatives of the area's Chantries, although in instances where a old and powerful Chantry dominates an area, it may also dominate the Council. The Council's main duties are to set and enforce local Protocols (the rules and regulations members of the Traditions live under, which vary from place to place), managing the local Ascension War, and handling diplomacy with outsiders. <br /> There's also a number of titles/positions that are part of the local structure. A Custos is a Mage charged with guarding and defending a local Chantry, Council, or individual or object of importance from mystical threat. (This can include anything from legendary Artifacts or gateways into the Umbra to a mundane individual who is believed to be Fated to play some important role in human destiny.)<br /> A Deacon is someone who assists Council members in their duties, often while being groomed for future leadership positions. Though some develop an overinflated sense of self-importance and become annoying antagonists for PC cabals. <br /> A Gardener is a Mage tasked with the care and preservation of a Wellspring, a place where Quintessence naturally bubbles up into the world, providing a valuable resource for a Chantry or Council.<br /> A Herald is a diplomat among the Traditions, usually in service to a Chantry or Council, but potentially also to a major Tradition faction, the Grand Council, or some regional collective (the Web of Faith, for instance).<br /> A Questor is a Mage tasked with the investigation of a specific event, individual or object. This frequently involves investigations of corruption, murder, or treason, as well as tracking down lost talismans, grimoires, or persons. <br /> A Rogue is a Tradition mage who has been expelled or ostracized from Mage society, usually as a result of protocol violation or political conflict. <br /> A Veilkeeper is a Mage tasked with maintaining the Veil of Secrecy, upholding the safety and security of Chantries and Cabals from mundane or Technocratic discovery, and protecting mundane bystanders from the dangers of the Awakened world. <br /> <br /> On a larger level, I borrowed the factions from Mage The Awakened for subsections within the Traditions. <br /> The Adamantine Arrow are those that focus on carrying out the Ascension War against both the Technocracy and the Nephandi. <br /> The Mysterium are scholars and seekers trying to develop a better understanding of reality, the world, and its hidden secrets. <br /> The Silver Ladder are those who focus on developing power and influence within the mundane world and its various sociopolitical power structures, as well as Tradition politics. <br /> The Guardians of the Veil are those who focus on upholding the Veil of Secrecy, and to a lesser degree the other protocols, protecting Tradition secrets from enemies and those who might be endangered by them. <br /> And the Rogue Council is a loose collection of various Rogues, Orphans and other Mages who pursue a number of purposes, including trying to recruit Orphans and Crafts into the Ascension War, protecting Orphans from the Ascension War, trying to reform or replace the existing Grand Council, turn themselves into a forth faction of the Ascension War, and others. <br /> <br /> There's also various regional factions, such as the Web of Faith and its counterparts in India, Africa, and other places.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-19T14:29:42-08:00","id":1361986,"title":"","content":"The Councils idea is something that I really like and might adopt as it provides a nice IC look into the organizational structure of the Traditions. The avatar and resonance ideas you have there are also very interesting and I may back pocket those.<br /> <br /> My group has never much cared for Awakening although I respect the distinct niche it has, so even if it's just for me and my vague musings, these are interesting ideas to see and get a look at.<br /> <br /> (Ah! I really need to see that movie at some point.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-19T15:04:25-08:00","id":1361996,"title":"","content":"Awakening, at least the 1st ed books I have, have a number of various ideas and concepts I've borrowed for Ascension. I also used a lot of other 1st ed CoD stuff in my other games. (Almost all of the Vigil factions have been repurposed, as everything from Tradition factions and sorcerer societies to hunter groups.)<br /> <br /> The Princess Bride is on my list of ten fantasy films from the 80s every gamer should see, along with Big Trouble in Little China, The Never-ending Story, Legend, The Dark Crystal, Labyrinth, The Black Cauldron, Timebandits, Return to Oz, and Clash of the Titans.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-19T16:28:14-08:00","id":1362011,"title":"","content":"Oh yeah, Vigil's factions have always seemed to me that a few exceptions aside are the neatest transmutation between Old &amp; New Worlds.<br /> <br /> A lot of those movies were before my time so while they're definitely on my list, I haven't had the chance to see many of them the way they were originally seen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-19T18:15:38-08:00","id":1362032,"title":"","content":"The trouble with the modern streaming model is that a lot of good films get claimed and sat on by HBO and other premium services, and never end up on broader streaming sites, or are considered too old or niche for a lot of sites to bother with. This is a stark contrast to the GenX 80s and 90s experience of going to one of a dozen local video rental places and finding not just the latest stuff but big name and obscure stuff from as far back as the 30s and 40s. (It's probably a big part of why Werewolf had such a big influence from 70s and 80s horror and scifi.)<br /> <br /> My 80s sci fi list is The Terminator, Aliens, Robocop, Brazil, TRON, The Adventures of Buckaroo Banzai Across the 8th Dimension, Dreamscape, Enemy Mine, The Last Starfighter, Alien Nation, The Running Man, Ghostbusters, The Brother from Another Planet, Liquid Sky, and Short Circuit.<br /> <br /> As far as Mage goes, my primary fiction influences include (books) Mary Shelly's Frankenstein, Jim Butcher's Dresdon Files, Grant Morison's The Invisibles, Alan Moore's Promethea, Jamie Delano's Hellblazer, Clive Barker's The Great and Secret Show (among others), Lev Grossman's The Magicians, CE Murphy's The Walker Papers, a lot of William Gibson, Neil Stephenson, and Dean Koontz, (films) Lord of Illusions, Dark City, The Adjustment Bureau, The Craft, The Gift (2000), Pi, Fight Club, In the Mouth of Madness, The Ninth Gate, and The Prestige.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 01-19-2020, 09:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-19T20:08:49-08:00","id":1362043,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that's a pretty big issue. I think the terms I've heard thrown around here and there is commercial/digital Balkanization.<br /> <br /> I wish I could say I'd absorbed all of those mediums, but I cannot. I will say that most that I have seen are some of my favorites an Hellblazer has been one of my favorite comics for a solid while.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-20T14:45:25-08:00","id":1362236,"title":"","content":"Another side effect of my focus on local issues was that I had to figure out just how the Traditions organize themselves at a local level. I wanted the various members of each Tradition to still come together and focus on the aspects of the Tradition, its paradigm and culture, even if they were scattered across multiple Cabals and Chantries. The places this happens can be Chantries that happen to cater to a single Tradition, or just the private sanctum and property of one of the Tradition's local masters. <br /> <br /> Members of the Akashic Brotherhood come together in local schools, which can be anything from martial arts dojos or religious temples to dance studios and private social clubs. Here they engage in meditation, martial arts practice, crafts, philosophical debate and other facets of the Eight Fold Way of Do. <br /> <br /> The Celestial Chorus fellowship in prayer, debate, and activism as a congregation. The nature of the group is typically decided by the ranking elders, with more traditional groups favoring ritual masses while less conservative ones tend toward simple group meetings and studies. <br /> <br /> Local Cultists favor informal gatherings hosted by area Masters, in which members engage in philosophical discussion, sensual exploration, and shared vision quests in an effort to feel Lakashim and reach Ananda. The host will typically decide on the cultural parameters and methods of exploration. <br /> <br /> Neighboring Dreamspeakers form medicine societies (or its equivalent term in other continents) to preserve and pass on sacred knowledge, resolve internal political problems, and foster a stronger sense of community among its members. While these societies have traditionally reflected the dominant local cultures, modern migrations and upheavals leave many areas having to balance multiple, sometimes conflicting, cultural needs.<br /> <br /> Euthanatos often meet in secrecy, hidden from those who might misunderstand their ways. The meeting site and group rituals typically reflect the tastes and traditions of the eldest Master or the majority of Thanatoics present. Temples, caves, and private drawing rooms are the most common locations. These courts offer mystical and ethical instruction, and well as discussion and debate on issues that impact the Great Wheel (including assassinations). <br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes is the most hierarchal of the Traditions, with formal meeting Chantries, mandatory monthly meetings, official rules of etiquette and order, and a tightly controlled agenda of lectures, debates, duels, and official declarations. <br /> <br /> Etherites communicate, debate, and feud with each other in the pages of their private journal and newsletter Paradigma. On a more local level, individuals will host parties and symposiums to show off their latest inventions, argue over competing theories, debate the events of the day, and make efforts at united political maneuvers. <br /> <br /> Local Verbena gather in covens, preferably consisting of a sacred number of members (3, 7, 9, or 13), in order to conduct sacred rituals, pass on ancient lore, and tend to the local Tree of Life. They also attempt to resolve internal disputes, plan for mutual protection, and coordinate future social and political activities. <br /> <br /> Almost all Virtual Adept interaction takes place online, either in the mundane deep web or the mystical Digital Web. Real world &quot;meat&quot; meetings tend toward a level of paranoia reminiscent of Cold War spycraft. Most groups run as democratic meritocracies, but they are frequently prone to passionate arguments and rampant egotism. <br /> <br /> Local Hollow Ones tend to congregate regularly at whatever nightclub, bookstore, coffee shop, or other hangout that is currently in favor. There they jockey mercilessly for status and prestige while claiming not to be interested in such things. On occasion, issues of actual importance may be settled. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-20T14:58:38-08:00","id":1362245,"title":"","content":"On a more mechanical side of things, I ended up adapting Awakening's Sphere set up, with the whole Knowing, Compelling, Unveiling, Ruling, Shielding, etc. set up. When I sat down and worked it out, I found that I had a better grasp of just what each Sphere could do at each level, that the first levels were able to do more, and that fifth levels could now create and destroy almost anything. <br /> <br /> I also refer to Correspondence as Connection, and Entropy as Fate. I think these terms convey what those spheres are about more coherently and clearly. <br /> <br /> And I started calling Spheres &quot;Keys&quot;, just because I like that name better (the Keys to reality). <br /> <br /> In addition to the Tradition's specialty Sphere/Key, I also have players pick a favored Sphere/Key that gets the same experience point discount.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-20T15:22:11-08:00","id":1362252,"title":"","content":"I really appreciate your insights and ideas here about the culture and behavior of the various traditions as that's always been the neat and potatoes of what's drawn me to the World of Darkness, given I'm not always the best person at handling crunch.<br /> <br /> I really do enjoy thinking about how these mystical cultures present and behave realistically within the setting and how they interact with one another.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-20T15:56:08-08:00","id":1362257,"title":"","content":"A lot of this developed just from the question of &quot;what to Mages do during an average week?&quot; Even D&amp;D characters have downtime a life outside of the adventuring party. All those backgrounds like Allies, Contacts, Mentor, Resources, Sanctum, and the like have to correspond to a person, place, or thing in the immediate setting. Lone Wolves, with no connections or ties to anyone or anything, work much better in fiction (the Jack Reacher novels, for example) than they do in an RPG. A bunch of people who have no lives outside their little group and the adventures they go on tend to be pretty boring, at least in my opinion. The more plugged in my characters are to the world immediately around them, the more skin they have in the game as far as the Ascension War and other parts of the setting goes. I like dealing with things like what happens when a Mage tries to balance being a Mage with also being a spouse and parent, or trying to be an active part of their community. These also put a more personal face on the Ascension War. If you have kids, the idea of them being co-opted by the Technocracy, either just from mundane compulsory state education, or being actively brainwashed and turned against you, can be utterly terrifying. <br /> <br /> And on the subject of backgrounds, I tried to pare them down to just ten, largely for the same reason I did with Abilities. I want those starting points to go as far as possible and make for characters with a lot of background ties. I have five mundane ones (that are roughly the same for all my games), Allies, Contacts, Followers, Influence, and Resources, and then five specific to the supernatural subculture, Library, Mentor, Sanctum, Status (a measure of one's place within the supernatural subculture's political sphere), and Talisman. For the Chantry, if the Cabal has one, I do it as a pooled background, with one point per person in the Cabal (and additional points if they choose to spend them on it), that can be spent on size, security, location, and other features. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-20T20:39:18-08:00","id":1362328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Grumpy RPG Reviews</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1361533#post1361533\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>The Nephandi are winning the Ascension War and the situation has long past the point of no return.</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div> I'm not criticizing this construction at all--it's <i>your</i> head canon/home brew, first and foremost (so who am I, or is anyone, to say you're wrong)--but I'm baffled by the idea that anyone would want to play that kind of setting. I mean, I'm not <i>surprised</i> by it at this point, because I've been in the hobby, and more to the point, playing WoD games, since like 1996 (when I was like 14), so I've understood that some people want to play the utterly bleak, all-hope-is-lost game. The first time I read <i>Kult</i> (can't remember which edition at this point--it was in college), I was just blown away--shocked that this was a setting someone would want to inhabit for (theoretically) <i>fun</i>.<br /> <br /> Again, I don't doubt that people genuinely and sincerely enjoy this kind of game, because there is clearly a large swath of the fandom (in this genre of RPG particularly) that regularly takes time from their adult lives to get together with friends and play these games, but I have a hard time wrapping my head around how great anyone's life could be that the fictional world they'd want to RP in would be <i>such</i> a bummer. I mean, I would understand playing the games as a form therapy or something, to work through issues that are easier to confront &quot;sideways&quot; as opposed &quot;head on&quot;, or perhaps playing the game as a kind of art object, or to raise your mindfulness about the suffering of others in the real world by inhabiting the skin of someone less fortunate, privileged, etc., than yourself (and I think this is something that LARP is actually used for in Europe)--but I want to understand where the <i>fun </i>is. And if the answer is &quot;it's just fun to me and I can't really understand it&quot;, I will 1000% accept that, but if you think you could put it into words at all, I'd definitely be very interested in reading your thoughts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-20T20:59:05-08:00","id":1362331,"title":"","content":"I also use something along the lines of the Gnosis paradigm (where the power stat represents a deep understanding of the mysteries of the world); but I call it Enlightenment, and present it as an alternative to Arete rather than a replacement for it. There are tangible differences between a mage with Enlightenment and a mage with Arete: those with Enlightenment don't get to Work Without Tools or Surpass their Instruments, but get to select Instruments that they master at the same rate that mages with Arete choose to surpass them; a mastered Instrument is just like a surpassed Instrument except that you can't do without it. On the other hand, their Enlightenment isn't capped by Willpower; they don't need to expend Willpower or personal Quintessence for the act of creating a Device/Talisman or Principia/Grimoire the way that mages with Arete do; and they also have a slightly easier time teaching magical tricks to Sleepers who have a compatible Focus (the Technocracy's Extraordinary Citizens and their less tech-focused counterparts).<br /> <br /> Technocrats are, almost to a man, Enlightened rather than Awakened (i.e., have Enlightenment rather than Arete); the handful of exceptions are most likely to be found among the Order of Reason Syndicate's SPD (see my earlier post for how I've changed it), and even there they're a minority. But Enlightenment isn't <i>exclusive to</i> the Union, or even to technomancers; though it's more common among technomancers than it is among mystics. The Traditions heavily lean toward Arete, with the exceptions of the Etherites and Virtual Adepts. Disparates vary; though they tend to lean toward Arete.<br /> <br /> I haven't actually done this; but I've considered making Faith a third variant of Arete, granting both the benefits and drawbacks of the True Faith Merit as well as the benefits and drawbacks of Arete. I haven't implemented it yet because I'm still not fully comfortable with the implementation.<br /> <br /> I also don't use Sorcery in my games. Instead, acolytes and extraordinary citizens can be taught individual rotes resulting in something along the lines of the sorcerers found in <b> Gods and Monsters</b>. As mentioned above, the Enlightened have a slight edge in training their adepts (with an emphasis on \u201cslight\u201d).<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> The main motivation for this is that I don't like the implicit \u201cthe Technocracy isn't just dangerous; they're also fundamentally deluded and/or deceitful about the nature of reality\u201d. This split between mechanically distinct Arete and Enlightenment (and maybe Faith) is an attempt to address that: the Enlightened see a universe with an underlying Truth to it, and their Science is an extrapolation of that Truth: careful use of their Instruments to exploit edge cases of how the world works lets them achieve results that seem impossible. And because of the benefits Enlightenment provides that Arete doesn't, a strong case can be made that they very well might be <i>right</i>. It's far from a sure thing, mind you; it's also possible that they're wrong and that the Traditions' view that We're all Gods in Disguise is right. And it's also possible that they're both wrong, like the tale of the Blind Men and the Elephant. I make a point of not answering that question \u2014 not for the players, and not even for myself.<br /> <br /> In a similar way, I make a point not to define precisely what an Avatar is, beyond a few characteristics it has. Further definition is left to each mage's Focus. This includes whether or not an Avatar is inherently part of a mage's soul or is something that gets grafted on when the mage Awakens/Enlightens/whatever. I personally assume that it's always there, just Sleeping until the Mage Awakens; but that's because of my view of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/798518-the-nature-of-the-consensus\">what the Consensus is</a>.<br /> <br /> That said, I like the alignments of various Natures to different Essences. It's a less radical version of something that I've been considering for a while now: jettisoning Nature and Demeanor (which is essentially a Vampire thing, given its heavy emphasis on intrigue) in favor of something more in tune with the nature of Mage. Werewolf and Changeling each have their own examples of how to replace Nature and Demeanor with something else. So far, my thoughts for Mage have been to replace the Nature-and-Demeanor system with a Paradigm-and-Essence system, where your choice of (M20-style) Paradigm earns you Willpower whenever you actively affirm the philosophy that the Paradigm represents; and your Essence likewise provides some sort of reward when you act in accord with it.<br /> <br /> In this regard, Essence might become a bit like Werewolf's Auspices, where every Garou regains Willpower in a manner consistent with one of the five Auspices. That said, there's something to be said for giving each Essence numerous Archetypes that replace Nature.<br /> <br /> As for the relationship between Essence and Resonance: I use the M20 system of Essence briefly sketched in M20 and detailed in Book of Secrets, with the four types of Resonance loosely coupled to Essence: Questing Avatars tend to push for actions that lead to Devotional Resonance; Primordial Avatars favor activities that generate Elemental Resonance; Pattern Avatars tend toward Stabilizing Resonance; and Dynamic Avatars foster Temperamental Resonance. (Note: these are <i>loose</i> associations. The mechanism, such as it is, is that your Avatar attempts to influence you toward certain kinds of actions \u2014 see the last paragraph about replacing Nature and Demeanor with something at least partially Essence-based \u2014 and those actions tend to produce the corresponding type of Resonance. For instance: if you have a Questing Avatar, you'll be getting Willpower rewards whenever your actions generate Devotional Resonance.)<br /> <br /> In keeping with this, I also use a different breakdown for Quiet: a four-way breakdown that aligns with the four Essences instead of a three-way breakdown aligning with the Metaphysical Trinity. This lets me factor the different types of Resonance into the rules for what kind of Quiet you experience more easily. You still have Denial and Madness, which correspond to Stabilizing and Temperamental Resonance, respectively; but Morbidity goes away. Instead, Elemental Resonance (such as Jhor) tends to promote Obsession (including, but not limited to, obsession with death: Morbidity is a subset of Obsession) while Devotional Resonance tends to promote Hubris.<br /> <br /> And just like there are Marauders who have succumbed to Madness and a recent phenomenon of \u201cMarauders\u201d who have succumbed to Denial (more or less the Negative Men from Book of Secrets, though I tweak them slightly), it's possible that there are \u201cMarauders\u201d who have succumbed to Obsession or Hubris. Possible, I say, because I haven't yet worked out the appropriate mechanics for them; so they're still largely hypothetical.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-14-2023, 03:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-21T08:44:18-08:00","id":1362415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1362328#post1362328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I'm not criticizing this construction at all--it's <i>your</i> head canon/home brew, first and foremost (so who am I, or is anyone, to say you're wrong)--but I'm baffled by the idea that anyone would want to play that kind of setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would not call it a hopeless setting, just one where the PCs cannot safely rely on any of the built-in social or political institutions. They cannot safely rely on the Traditions, or the Technocracy, to either save them if the going gets tough, or to do the right thing when the time comes. It all comes down to the PCs - they are on their own. This is not the same thing as saying things are really hopeless, but it does mean adjusting your expectations.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-21T09:21:08-08:00","id":1362422,"title":"","content":"To me, the line that makes me think \u201chopeless setting\u201d is \u201cpast the point of no return\u201d: the Traditions and Technocracy are so far gone in their respective corruption that there's no saving them. The Nephandi aren't just winning; they've already <i>won</i> \u2014 it's just that the final steps to ultimate destruction haven't yet finished playing out. The world has already crossed a line from which there's no coming back, and the end result is going to be the same no matter what. <i>That's</i> about as bleak and hopeless as things get. <br /> <br /> There's a difference between \u201cthe Traditions and Technocracy have been compromised, and can't be trusted or relied upon as they are now\u201d and \u201cthey're so far gone that there's no saving them\u201d. The former is desperate but has a ray of hope; the latter is just bleak.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-21T14:46:24-08:00","id":1362525,"title":"","content":"<b>House Canon/Setting Themes</b><br /> Generally, we follow DA:M and the Revised supplements in terms of setting history. The Craftmasons and Cabal of Pure Thought split off from their respective Fellowships around the 13th century, etc.<br /> <br /> When we have threads like this, I tend to think that I &quot;agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> from the opposite direction.&quot; I think of paradigms <i>as </i>paradigms (which is to say, as models). The Union's paradigm isn't delusional - there's a lot of ways to talk about your Avatar, and all mages think reality has rules. Rather, the Union's unusual position comes from its doctrinal rigidity (&quot;everyone must conform to our model&quot;) and the inner circle's desire to suppress any discovery (scientific or sorcerous) which threatens its power - neither of these are necessary parts of a Unionist's paradigm. But I very specifically don't draw any meaningful or mechanical distinction between science and magic.<br /> <br /> We have two long-running &quot;continuities&quot; in which our chronicles and one-shots are run. They have slightly different thematic elements, with the metaphysical trinity being more important in one (and the moral compass being more concerned with balance than niceness) while the other is more Manichean and Christian in tone (primarily used for one-shots in the Colonial Americas and other historical eras.) In the latter, we also use the old proposed Victorian Mage idea about the Trads/Conventions being deep in the background, so the various characters aren't really privy to the Ascension War or Horizon Realms, etc.<br /> <br /> We tend to run mono-Trad oneshots, often in historical eras, so the &quot;splats&quot; we use for those tend to be subfactions. I've done some work on these to broaden their scope or make them more interesting, so that you can have a diverse cast within the bounds of a single Trad.<br /> <br /> There are some historical groups we've added, early fellowships which didn't directly reform into a Trad or Convention. Some of these are envisioned as being fairly influential even though they never achieved political power - basically, these groups live on through their discoveries, iconography, and rotes, instead of as a political unit.<br /> <br /> <b>Rules Stuff</b><br /> We've been adopting the translation guides and nWoD rules over time, and now basically use them full-time. I have a nauseatingly large &quot;playbook&quot; for running Ascension using a version of Awakening 2e.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":1653,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","user":"Five Eyes","date":". 01-21-2020, 09:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-22T08:33:24-08:00","id":1362656,"title":"","content":"Honestly I kind of go the opposite direction. True Nephandi were wiped out centuries ago, and the word is more or less the magical communities equivalent to Terrorist in that it's basically just an excuse to stop pulling punches and do things that would otherwise be considered too far. While also being a convenient way to disavow more extreme elements.<br /> <br /> Euthanatos Master blows up an Orphanage? Clearly a Nephandi, not truely part of our group. The Standard Nephandi Gilgul, is largely to prevent the Avatar from revealing this in the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T14:11:13-08:00","id":1362951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Five Eyes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1362525#post1362525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I very specifically don't draw any meaningful or mechanical distinction between science and magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>This may be a nitpick; but so be it. For what it's worth, I don't want to draw any meaningful or mechanical distinction between science or magic, either. Not per se. There's a reason why I say that there are mystics with Enlightenment and technomancers with Arete: I explicitly want to encourage the notion that Enlightenment vs. Arete isn't Science vs. Magic. <br /> <br /> But yeah; we very much \u201cagree from opposite directions\u201d: we both see the same problem with the game as presented, but opt for contradictory solutions to it. The problem is that there are de facto distinctions between science and magic in the game that are somewhat inconsistent. My solution is to develop them further; your solution is to remove them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T18:49:00-08:00","id":1362986,"title":"","content":"Since my chantries are all earth based, I had to fiddle with a few of the famous ones, and ended up inventing a few to serve as central chantries for some of the Traditions. <br /> The Akashic Brotherhood's Akashic Library remains mostly unchanged; it's a construct of the mind - and of Mind magic - that exists within the minds of all members of the Tradition. And because that includes both past and future members, it is entirely possible for those with enough understanding of Time magic to meet and converse with Brothers from other eras. (Their more normal chantries include the Temple of Hidden Truth in Tibet, the Eight Dragons Trading Company in Singapore, and the Golden Drum School in Sao Louis Brazil.)<br /> <br /> The Celestial Chorus's Curia and Pontifex Maximus operate out of the Temple of the Sun, originally founded by Mentu-hetep in ancient Egypt, and now co-located in sites in Rome, Iran, Ethiopia, Peru, Japan, Arizona, and Australia. (Others include the Temple of Brahma's Footprint in India, the Hidden Catacombs beneath Jerusalem, and the Theban Club in Philadelphia.)<br /> <br /> Balador is a combination French Salon, &quot;Oriental&quot; pleasure garden, and modern nightclub that exists outside the flow of time and space, serving as the home of the Shakti Divya, Marianna, the Cult of Ecstasy's spiritual heart, and a popular gathering place for those Awakened who know how to get on the guest list. (Others include the Hidden Dream Village of Nepal, the Temple of Sapho in Greece, the Rose Luck Club in Seoul, South Korea, and the nomadic Coyote Caravan of the American Southwest.) <br /> <br /> During the Grand Convocation, the Dreamspeakers established the House of the Clan Mothers (named in honor of Naioba), a grand meeting lodge that exists simultaneously in thirteen locations around the globe. These locations are determined by the ritual placement of thirteen sacred artifacts, each one entrusted to a different Dreamspeaker Master or Archmage. (Other important sites include Boolarung, an Australian songline that takes those who follow it outside of mundane space and time, the hidden West African village of Olodumare, the Great Turtle Lodge in Quebec, and the Temple of the Eternal Hearth in Greece.)<br /> <br /> Vajra Coumatha, the Eternal Crossroads, is both a construct of the mind and a physical place outside of mundane time and space that can only be reached by those who are fated to reach it. It has served as a sacred meeting place for Thanatoic Masters for countless centuries. (More conventional chantries include the Great Wheel Marabout in the foothills of the Himalayas, Southern Africa's Ghost Citadel, Iceland's Well of Mimir, and London's Locksmiths' Club.)<br /> <br /> For centuries, the ancient fortress chantry of Doissetep was the Order of Hermes's pride, the heart of the Order, and meeting place of the Grand Council. Its recent destruction<sup>1</sup> has left the Order in something of an upheaval. (Surviving strongholds include Milan's Harco Piazza, the secret library of Seshat in Alexandria, Egypt, the Red Griffin Inn in England, and the Pyrander Club in Washington, DC.)<br /> <br /> Established in the 19th century, Victoria Station is a secret hidden part of London's Underground that uses EtherTube transportation systems to connect to locations around the world. It is host to the head offices and publishing center for Paradigma, and meeting place for the Tradition's ruling Council of Deans. (Other places of note include the converted New York City speak easy that now serves as the exclusive Gibson Club, the Kimota Observatory in Australia's Outback, California's Time-Stop Laboratories, and the Hidden Island of Doctor X in the Pacific Ocean.)<br /> <br /> Arcadian Grove is the most sacred of sacred groves, believed to date back to the time of the Wyck and only accessible by those initiated into the ways of the Wyck and familiar with the secret hidden paths that lead to it. Leaders of the Verbena have met there since prehistory. (Other places of power include Woodwich Downs, the family estate of Lady Charlotte Quay in England, the Temple of Demeter in Greece, Tara Plantation in Georgia, and the Cromby Farm in Washington State.)<br /> <br /> The Crystal Palace is one of the oldest and most secure fortresses in the Digital Web, and a cornerstone of the Virtual Adepts since it was established in the 1940s. It is the center of the Adepts' and even the Traditions' efforts in the Web, and open to any Virtual Adept once they've been initiated. Many areas of the Palace, however, require additional security codes and clearances to access. (Other Digital sites include the youth hipster hangout known as the Crashpad, the mysterious safe-house Dark Side, and the techno-geek paradise of Vulcan. A rare meat-world chantry of note is The Bunker, a hidden doomsday shelter at an unidentified location in the Nevada desert.)<br /> <br /> The Hollow Ones lack any real central chantry, but a number of locals have gained fame - or infamy - among those in the know. These include the Waydown, the wandering outlaw rave of the greater San Fransisco Bay Area, the haunted Chicago mansion known as the Sepulcher, and the underground London sex shop and nightclub Guilty Pleasures. But the most famous is Grimme's Occult Specialty Shoppe, a small and colorful curiosity that seems to exist simultaneously in dozens of cities around the western world. <br /> <br /> With the recent destruction of Doissetep, there was (in my games) a need to establish a new meeting place for the Grand Council and create a symbol of hope for Mages going forward in the Ascension War. That place is Horizon, the brain child of Archmage Porthos Fitz-Empress. My default setting for it is outside of Seattle, but I can easily move it to wherever I feel like setting my games. This isn't the Horizon of the sourcebook by that name, but a recent construction that takes full advantage of Correspondence and other magics to fit a massive amount of material into a small space. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T21:17:53-08:00","id":1363010,"title":"","content":"Yo those are all some really cool places! I need to get some more practical stuff like this together but mage has always been one of those things that I have a trickier time with getting myself into the practical matters because there is such a broad spectrum that it can operate at, from the most pariah and guerrilla street game to the most highflying and quintessence flinging umbral game.<br /> <br /> Honestly it's a big reason about why I like to put out threads like these to reap ideas and thoughts together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T22:38:59-08:00","id":1363022,"title":"","content":"Those are some great ideas for chantries. I'll be stealin' those.<br /> <br /> For the Nephandi, I tend to run real Nephandi as very rare (and very dangerous), and present the Trad and Techno leadership as being in a bit of denial about them seeping their way back into the world. The victory in WWII is part of the leadership's &quot;mythology&quot; and so they're reluctant to admit that they might not have, y'know, solved the problem forever. Some of this is infiltration and corruption, but it's primarily just pride and wishful thinking.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> - Yeah, fair clarification.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-23T23:09:18-08:00","id":1363024,"title":"","content":"I'm in favor of the notion that a lot of so-called Nephandi sightings are fake, along the lines of what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11744\" data-vbnamecard=\"11744\">Enginseer-42</a> is describing \u2014 but <i>not</i> because the Nephandi are almost entirely gone. No; it's because calling someone a Nephandus is an effective smear tactic. And every time a supposed Nephandic encounter is proven to be a cynical ploy, it makes it less likely that people will believe you the next time. I won't go so far as to say that the Nephandi <i>encourage</i> such behavior; but it's awfully convenient for them when it happens. Especially since, these days, a Nephandus' M.O. is frequently to become your best friend and convince <i>you</i> to do increasingly horrific things \u2014 all for good and noble reasons, at least to start out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-25T19:57:46-08:00","id":1363392,"title":"","content":"I enjoy the Sons of Ether quite a bit, however my take on them differs slightly from canon.<br /> <br /> They are still scientists and explorers seeking to understand the true nature of reality while rebelling against the tyrannical conformity of the Technocracy, inspired by the ancient works of Aretus and his writings about lost Atlantis (ie, the world that existed before the &quot;shattering&quot; that led to the current state of reality), and based on the philosophy that Reality is stranger than one can imagine, as it is infused with Ether, the very material of the Tapestry itself, which can be manipulated through proper use of Science and ingenuity. However, they've never been part of the Technocracy, not even in the Victorian period. (Though certain strains of their history do tie in with the medieval Craftmasons.) In my games, when the Technocracy was formed in 1851, a number of those enlightened scientists invited rejected the idea that the masses - and with it their science and technology - needed to be controlled and shepherded by their elite betters. To these dissenters, this mindset flew in the very face of open and honest scientific inquire and the advancement of mankind's understanding of the universe and their place in it. They formed their own Scientific society, The Electrodyne Engineers. After The Great War and the colossal failure of alleged Technocratic expertise in managing human politics and conflict (as well as the noble but doomed quixotic effort by Czar Vargo to avert the war through force), the Engineers, through one of their leaders and heroes Doc Eon, form an alliance with the Nine Mystical Traditions, formally joining in the 1920s. (This brings there number back to nine, as I don't have the Ahl I Batin leaving until WW2.)<br /> <br /> The name is a matter of some contention within the Tradition. Traditionalists demand on preserving the &quot;Sons of&quot; name that the group created to mark its formal alliance with the Traditions, however, since the rise of second wave feminism in the 1960s, there have been those advocating (and demanding) a change in the name. Many younger members in the 21st centuries refer to themselves as the Society of Ether, but even this isn't enough to satisfy some. (One reason I keep the &quot;Sons&quot; part of the name has to do with one of the factions I mention below.)<br /> <br /> Their key Sphere is still Matter, and one of the reasons all of them have Matter 1 to start with is that part of their initiation is to build their first set of Ether Goggles, allowing them to better observe Ether and its effects on the material world. These Ether Goggles become one of their first foci. Other common foci tend to involve scientific meters and tools, specialized alloys and chemicals, experimental weapons systems and vehicles, strange mental disciplines and exercise programs, bizarre mathematical and chemical formula, and mind boggling engineering schematics and blue prints. Almost all of the above tend to be self-designed and built. Their sanctums are mostly private labs and workshops, but also include exclusive social clubs and secret hidden lairs. Their acolytes tend to be extremely openminded and courageous lab assistants, personal assistants who can take care of all the boring stuff that distracts one from Science!, would be sidekicks to science adventurers, and engineered robots and bio-forms. The weakness of the Tradition lies in their excessive enthusiasm and eccentricity. (Basic concepts: Daredevil test pilot, Eugenic superman, Obsessive cryptozoological conservationist, Steampunk adventuress, Time-traveling doctor.)<br /> <br /> My Etherites have four main factions, each with a number of subsections.<br /> <br /> <b>L'Grand Collegium</b> serves as the primary organizational body of the Sons of Ether. Organized along the lines of a college or academy, they are responsible for overseeing and enforcing the Protocols of Science (the rules and guidelines that govern the Tradition), recruiting and training new Scientists, and conducting diplomacy with the other Traditions and other outsiders.<ul><li><i>L'Academie Ether</i> - devoted to the sacred and often thankless task of recruiting and teaching the next generation of young Etherites, members embed themselves in various schools, universities, research labs, and technical facilities to try and find promising minds before the Technocracy can corrupt them; They teach their charges the basics of Etheric Science, ethics, history, and Ascension War survival skills.</li> <li><i>Paradigma Publications</i> - the publication and propaganda arm of the Collegium and the Tradition as a whole, maintaining quasi-hidden websites and running a print on demand book service; Members work as reporters, correspondents, and investigators around the world and beyond, filing news reports, public interest pieces, and editorials.</li> <li><i>Council for the Enforcement of Scientific Ethics</i> - policing the Tradition for the taint and infiltration of the Nephandi, the Technocracy, and other malign influences, as well as more mundane cases of insanity, criminal behavior, and violation of the Protocols of Science; Members are expected to be skilled Scientists as well as having a working understanding of philosophy, law, forensics, and self-defense.</li> <li><i>Right and Honourable Keepers of Etheric Tradition</i> - a gentlemen's club and political society for arch-traditionalists devoted to preserving Victorian and pre-War mores of etiquette, ethics and values; This includes the ideals of honour, courage, and chivalry, as well as traditions of formal wear, high tea, and dueling; Members firmly believe in such outmoded concepts as duty, patriotism, and good manners while insisting on suitably elegant stylings - Steampunk, Art Deco, and Silver Age Science Fiction - to one's Scientific creations. (Though as younger members join, optimistic 1980s styles are becoming acceptable as well.) The current leader of the society is the bi-centenarian engineer, explorer, and metallurgical alchemist Professor Lord Primium.</li> <li><i>Society for a Better Tomorrow</i> - utopian activists working to use Science! for the betterment of all humankind, eliminating need and want, disease, war, poverty, and misery; Members want to create a world of social justice and equality, but are prone to a form of passive-aggressive pushiness and often ignoring human nature.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Globe-trotting Paradigma reporter; Utopian idealists trying to design the City of Tomorrow; Victorian throwback obsessed with decorum and manners; Rookie agent of the Science Police; Smooth-talking academic recruiter; Over-worked teacher's aide; Well-respected traditionalists carrying on a secret romance with a progressive anarchist.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Ethernautical Society</b> are daring explorers eager to push forward the boundaries of knowledge and possibility into new dimensions, lost lands, and strange frontiers. These undiscovered countries must be protected from exploitation and harm by the Technocracy and other threats. Members are constantly seeking new and exiting avenues of exploration and adventures into the furthest secret corners of Reality.<ul><li><i>The Cryptozoological Conservation Committee</i> - a collective of naturalists, folklorist, and veterinarians dedicated to studying and preserving Bygones and their habitats; They are ready, willing, and able to trek to the most remote and dangerous corners of the globe for their charges.</li> <li><i>The Etherjammers</i> - taking their name from the umbral ship of the legendary Pulp Era explorer and adventurer Captain Tiberius, they work in teams (Cabals) to create and man Etherships to cross the Gauntlet and explore the hidden corners and outermost reaches of the Otherworlds; Members specialize in cosmic and cross dimensional navigation, xenobiology and xenopsychology, Ethernautical engineering, and defensive combat. (These Etherships and their crews are more akin to something like Firefly's Serenity or Star Wars's Millennium Falcon rather Star Trek's various ships; small ships with small, tightly knit Cabal crews rather than massive battleships.)</li> <li><i>The Metaphysical Research Society</i> - investigating the hidden mysteries and untapped potentials of the human mind; Members specialize in psychobiology, metaphysics, psychogenesis, psychometrics, and psychochemistry, experimenting with mind-altering chemicals, meditation techniques, and methods of mental projection; Some of their research also involves trying to help those Etherites who have fallen into mental instability, as well as trying to gain a better understanding of Marauders and Changelings.</li> <li><i>The College of Chronoarchaeology</i> - as much a part of the Collegium as the Society, they delve into the mysteries of the past and the future, with a special focus on secret history, conspiracy theories, and the true nature of the universe's creation (and possible end), as well as the study of future technology and events, and unlocking the secrets of time travel. Their headquarters is the Time-Stop Laboratories in Southern California.</li> <li><i>Cyber-Liberte</i> - cybernaut explorers of the Digital Web and of the mundane internet; Strongly allied with the Virtual Adepts, they are staunch advocates of free speech and freedom of information; The group has always had a strange affection for both pirate and French Revolution motifs, frequently styling their Web avatars with buccaneer or pre-Jacobian themes, and seeing themselves as adventurers on the high seas and back alleys of the Digital Web, preying on the data of the Technocracy; Members tend to design and build their own computers and VR rigs, some of which confound even the Virtual Adepts.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Professional Bigfoot hunter; Time-lost anachronism trying to find his way home; Swashbuckling pirate queen of the space lanes; Self-proclaimed Master of Mental Mysteries; Conspiratorial historian determined to expose The Truth; Radical ecological terrorist defending Bygone habitats; Mentally unstable necro-naut.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Adventurers' Club</b> are a social organization for those staunchly dedicated to the use of Science! in the active and resolute battle against evil and injustice. Its members act as detectives, science heroes, and vigilantes, battling the foes with high tech wizardry and a firm punch to the jaw. Their headquarters and central meeting place is the Gibson Club, a former New York City speakeasy that was converted into a private club in the 1930s.<ul><li><i>The Crimebusters</i> - focused on waging a war against mundane crime and corruption, as well as that resulting from supernatural influences, primarily at a local street level, but some target white collar crime and government corruption; Members favor a mix of applied psychological warfare and custom made high-tech weaponry and gadgets; While many of the original members were willing to take the lives of criminals with little hesitation, there has been.a divide on the issue of lethal justice for the past several decades.</li> <li><i>The Guardians of Tomorrow</i> - futurists, prognosticators, and predictive scientists working to stop crises before they develop, sometimes with mixed results; They also work to foster a more optimistic and positive view of the future in mundane society, through media, technological development, and inspirational actions.</li> <li><i>The Exemplars</i> - a fringe faction of self-made supermen and women, touting their mental, physical, and moral superiority as worthy of emulation by the rest of humanity; While most are well meaning and genuine, their perceived arrogance tends to rub others the wrong way.</li> <li><i>The Ladies' Science Circle &amp; Adventure Society</i> - a long standing social club founded in the Victorian era for female Science! adventuresses, including detectives, reporters, aviators, explorers, swashbucklers of the stars, computer hackers, and other thrill seekers; They tirelessly work to maintain and publicize the historical achievements of female Etherites and Adventurers to help inspire future generations.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Annoyingly optimistic science fiction writer; Faceless urban vigilante trying to stay true to a code against killing; Square-jawed throw-back to the thrilling days of Shere Damn Manly Science Adventure!; Jet-setting detective with an endless bag of high-tech tricks; Obnoxiously paternalistic ubermensch; Mentally enhanced psychic seer; Complete fraud who made up all of his exploits.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Progressivist Alliance</b> is a loose collection of radicals, rebels, and revolutionaries united in their opposition to the dictates and limitations imposed by the Grand Collegium. They want to take their Science! and the world into strange, disturbing, and even dangerous directions.<ul><li><i>The League of Scientific Anarchism</i> - the core of the Alliance, radicals demanding a lessoning - if not outright abolishment - of the Protocols governing Scientific research and experimentation; They include sincere libertarians and anarchists, as well as mad scientists wishing to carry out their morally questionable research in peace.</li> <li><i>The Vargo Collective</i> - true believers in the vision of Czar Vargo, advocating the overthrow of all mundane governments and their replacement with an enlightened Etherite meritocracy (some also advocate the inclusion of select members of other Traditions), total global disarmament, the end of war, and the pursuit of Science! for purely peaceful and benevolent purposes; Since the 1960s, they've been divided over their adherence to Vargo's code against taking human life, with more radical members arguing for violent action against the Technocracy and other enemies.</li> <li><i>The Electrodyne Divas</i> - all womyn radical femynist movement firmly united in their opposition to the Tradition's outmoded and sexist phallocratic patriarchal society, beginning with the Tradition's name and moving down a very long list of grievances both real and imagined. Their outspoken and impressively intimidating leader is Dr. Amazonia, also a member of the Examplars and frequent publisher of scathing Paradigma editorials.</li> <li><i>The Monster Makers</i> - a small circle of fanatics obsessed with the creation of biological, mechanical, and spiritual monstrosities to unleash against the Technocracy, headless of collateral damage; Most of them have very short but interesting careers.</li> <li><i>The Galateans</i> - advocates for the rights and human treatment of artificially created lifeforms. including clones, androids, Prometheans, artificial intelligences, uplifted animals, and others (including mystical creations such as homunculi); While this movement tends to be socio-political and non-violent, some radicals take a much more direct approach in their activities.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Radical female-supremest Science Amazon; Radical anarchist with a death-ray; Fanatical Vargoite willing to kill for world peace; Seemingly normal Scientist with a secret basement full of monsters; Forthright political advocate for freedom and discovery; Modern-day Frankenstein trying to raise his creation properly; Mentally unstable delver into Things Man Was Not Meant To Know.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 01-26-2020, 11:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-25T20:27:41-08:00","id":1363399,"title":"","content":"My Etherites are very similar to the Etherites as officially described, which makes them very different from the popular conception of what they are. <br /> In my take on it, \u201cThe Ether\u201d isn't a substance, primarily; it's a symbol: it's a symbol of a martyred theory, a casualty of the unnecessary reductionism that the Union has been pushing ever since the Etherites jumped ship. Few Etherites hold the actual substance in any special esteem, other than for its symbolism. And in a positive sense, the Ether is a symbol of the limitless potential of Science. <br /> <br /> The Etherites aren't stuck in the past, either: they're not a Tradition of Steampunk enthusiasts, though they do respect the Maker culture that steampunk has fostered.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 03-28-2020, 11:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-25T21:08:40-08:00","id":1363402,"title":"","content":"My impression is that when Etherites talk about Ether, they are talking about something different from mundane ether or \u00e6ther. It's basically McGuffin-ite Bat-energy that doesn't exist in the real world (although theories about dark matter/energy/fluid and quantum field theory could be interpreted as mundane detection of Ether), with the name being a traditional term used because it's the common translation of what is in the ancient manuscript.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-25T23:16:14-08:00","id":1363413,"title":"","content":"<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The <i>Kitab al-Alacir</i> lays out the general philosophy of Ether Science. Everything exists, everything originates in The Ether. Humanity filters out most of the cosmos so that it makes sense; but limitless potential is always battering at the gates of the Consensus. Science draws from the well of the infinite to fashion a more complete model. Eventually, Sleepers will be able to understand the entire majesty of Creation; the Wall of Troy will encompass it all.<br /> <br /> <i>Sons of Ether Tradition Book, p.55</i></div><i>This</i> is the essence of the Etherites; not Ether. That said:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><b>The Eternal Ether</b><br /> The only common root is the Ether, the basic medium of the universe. Ether takes an infinite number of forms and occupies all space and time, but it is defined by what it is <i>not</i>. It isn't a mental construction, a substance or a force. It is the place from which everything \u2014 even Quintessence \u2014 rises into existence, and where everything goes when it departs. All matter and energy is conserved.<br /> <br /> Aside from the fundamental Ether (what Scientists often call the \u201cIdeal Ether\u201d), other \u201cEthers\u201d encompass partial states of material and metaphysical reality. Quintessence is held to be the \u201cpurest\u201d of these. It is wholly metaphysical, only existing when perceived and acting when acted upon. Contiguous Ether is more base, since it encompasses the physical properties of distance and the relationship between objects. Etherspace is a series of dimensions that lie outside of the Consensus's \u201cWall of Troy\u201d. It has physical features, but they lie outside normal human experience. Quantum Ether Theory holds that they are alternate universes that have developed due to humanity's rejection of certain cosmological principles.<br /> <br /> <i>Sons of Ether Tradition Book, p.55</i></div>This last point in particular ties into why the Sons of Ether call themselves that. They defected from the Union when the Void Engineers introduced their Tychoidean Cosmology model, which encouraged humanity's rejection of certain cosmological principles: it made it Union policy to embrace the divide between Conventional Space and Etherspace and to keep the Masses ignorant of the latter. Ultimately, the goal was to strengthen the barrier between the two to the point that Etherspace would be completely cut off from Conventional Space, and humanity could expand throughout Conventional Space without ever having to worry about intrusions from beyond. It was this dismissal of Etherspace and the underlying embrace of reductionism, not the Luminiferous Ether, that the Electrodyne Engineers took offense to and named themselves after when they left the Technocracy. <div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><b>True Ether</b><br /> If there is a \u201c10th Sphere\u201d for the Sons of Ether, it is their namesake. Ether is the fundament of the cosmos, and to discover its true nature underneath the phenomena it creates would surely raise a Scientist to the pinnacle of her calling. True Ether is more basic then even the Tellurian's Quintessential roots because it must include Paradox (which is not Quintessential, but reacts to Ether Science) and the methods of other Traditions. Ether holds the key to these alien secrets, and the Sons are eager to find it.<br /> <br /> It is said that to the real goal of all experiments is to touch this True Ether. The Sons' holy grail is a theory that explains all of the Ether's manifestations according to a single set of laws. This would describe the Ether fully \u2014 and with it, the true nature of the universe.<br /> <br /> <i>Sons of Ether Tradition Book, p.60</i></div>So yes, Ether is central to the Etherites \u2014 but not in the way most think. It's the ideal that they pursue, a goal of a grand unified theory of everything, not a substance or force that they manipulate. One <i>could</i> say that Matter and Forces can be thought of in terms of exploring the True Ether's manifestation through substances and forces, just as Correspondence is Contiguous Ether and Prime is Metaphysical Ether (and Entropy is arguably Quantum Ether, and Spirit is arguably fundamentally about Etherspace). Etheric Biology (the Life Sphere) has a tendency to incorporate notions such as Elan Vital, the idea that there <i>is</i> a \u201cLife Force\u201d. Instead of Etheric Biology, one might call this notion \u201cBiological Ether\u201d. (Likewise, Mind is generally envisioned in terms of \u201cmental energy fields\u201d, akin to Trinity Continuum's notion of Noetic Science.) And Time, of course, is Causal Ether (dealing with the fundamental nature of cause and effect) in the same way that Correspondence is Contiguous Ether. So, the Nine Spheres as seen by the Sons of Ether:<br /> <br /> Biological Ether (life energy)<br /> Causal Ether (a web of cause and effect)<br /> Contiguous Ether (connections that transcend distance)<br /> Energetic Ether (Luminiferous, Gravitic, etc.)<br /> Etherspace<br /> Material Ether<br /> Metaphysical Ether<br /> Noetic Ether (mental energy)<br /> Quantum Ether<br /> <br /> True Ether","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 03-28-2020, 10:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-26T09:22:39-08:00","id":1363459,"title":"","content":"As far as style goes, my take on the Sons of Ether is that for a bunch of scientists, they also tend to have a strong romantic streak to them. Besides the ethical devotion to honest scientific inquiry and discovery, and to expanding human potential, this also leads them to being more than a little flamboyant, or at least extremely enthusiastic about their pursuits and very attached to certain time periods that they either came of age in (the older ones) or have an attraction to as a symbol of the creativity and possibility of science that the Technocracy has stamped out (the younger retro ones). While some go full retro, I suspect most just favor incorporating certain elements into their designs or fashions. Those common styles likely being some of the following.<ul><li><i>Gothic-Punk</i>: Probably the most common, almost to the default, and those who appear &quot;normal&quot;. They dress like the average club crawler or young iconoclast, complete with piercings and tattoos, their gear looking like it started in a science lab but was finished in their garage workshop.</li> <li><i>New Wave Science</i>: Those in love with the 80s and its strange mix of bright optimism and internal anxieties. Taking a lot of cues from Buckaroo Banzai, Back to the Future, and even Devo, as well as some of the aspects of Cyberpunk.</li> <li><i>Analog Punk</i>: Grim and gritty do-it-yourself engineering of the pre-digital era, using vacuum tubes, transistors, and other older technology, largely as a backlash against how easily the Technocracy and its agents have been able to co-opt and abuse modern digital technology.</li> <li><i>Silver Age Science Fiction</i>: A favorite of utopians looking for a nice &quot;lean and clean&quot; look to their work, it also frequently tends to involve the use of Atomic Energy (or ATOMICS!) in some fashion. Flying cars and phasers are also popular.</li> <li><i>Diesel Punk</i>: Inspired by early German Expressionism film, WW1 and WW2 era engineering, and Film Noir, you can see some examples of it in parts of the Hellboy comics, Dark City, Sky Captain &amp; The World of Tomorrow, and Crimson Skies. Etherites who adopt this style will sometimes be mistaken for Hollow Ones.</li> <li><i>Art Deco Pulp</i>: A frequent favorite of the Adventurers, usually reflecting a devotion to the more humanistic styles of 20s, 30s and 40s architecture and design that was supplanted by the somewhat dehumanizing and oppressive styles like Brutalism and Functionalism.</li> <li><i>Retro-Victorian Steampunk</i>: One of the popular styles/fads for the past decade or so, possibly owing to a number of Etherites having been part of the subculture prior to Awakening and simply carrying it over.</li> </ul>There's also the possibility of &quot;Geek Chic&quot;, the current trend of pop culture to be full to the point of bloating with things that used to be part of &quot;nerd culture&quot; - comic book superheroes, video game merchandise, sci fi and fantasy franchises, anime, etc. - that are currently popular and hip because of big budget films, streaming series, and the like. However, this sort of thing may be rejected by some Etherites out of the belief that this is the Technocracy co-opting these things for their own purposes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-27T14:03:51-08:00","id":1363675,"title":"","content":"My favorite WoD headcanon<br /> 1: the reason the Fallon make no mention of Lilith is that [Jehova] did a hard reboot after he had to kick her out and a side effect of her eating of the forbidden fruit is she appears as a god to Angel's, and they can't tell the difference between Gods<br /> 2: the maiden from Revelations of the Dark Mother is Grandmother from orpheus <br /> 3: Lucifer wrote the Precepts of Damian to aim the Order of Reason at his enemies","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>there are no bad ideas, just ideas that have not been refined enough","edited":{"userid":1608,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1608-iamthesweeper","user":"IAmTheSweeper","date":". 01-27-2020, 05:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1608-iamthesweeper","name":"IAmTheSweeper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1608_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"121"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-30T04:32:48-08:00","id":1364132,"title":"","content":"I realize this stretches the definition of house rule, but I have some ideas for either a Powered by the Apocalypse or Forged in the Dark adaptation of Mage. Rather than relying on the Spheres, the stats would be based on the Essences, as would magic itself - magic wouldn't care about your level of understanding of the phenomena you are affecting, but does care about your level of understanding of the primal forces of statis, dynamism, and entropy (and possibly adding in a fourth for questing in PbtA, since four is a common minimum for number of attributes there). <br /> <br /> I also kind of want to adapt this with the Pillar system as a general sphere alternative - instead of using different pillars for aspects of your beliefs and the world around you, focus on those fundamentals.<br /> <br /> Separate note: indigo lost of the advantages and flaws from the MET system and probably would try to find a way to adapt them. Somehow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-30T07:00:02-08:00","id":1364150,"title":"","content":"Maybe instead of Essences, you could do Resonance or Synergies? The three Synergies are Dynamic, Static, and Entropic; the four Resonances are Devotional, Emotional, Elemental, and Stabilizing (corresponding loosely with the Questing, Dynamic, Primordial, and Pattern Essences, respectively). While I wouldn't call it \u201cunderstanding\u201d per se, there are systems available to give you dot ratings in either or both of these, along with flavors for more specificity. A setup where Magick is directly Resonance-based could be interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-21T05:55:40-08:00","id":1369345,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1361967#post1361967\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Guardians of the Veil are those who focus on upholding the Veil of Secrecy, and to a lesser degree the other protocols, protecting Tradition secrets from enemies and those who might be endangered by them.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm curious as to how you incorporated the Guardians into Ascension. I'm also thinking about using the orders in Ascension, but I find the Guardians the hardest to incorporate. Since they are largely based on the Lie, the way Paradox is perceived in Mage society and it's metaphysical importance and the way Mages relate to sleeper society. But those things are quiet different in Ascension and Awakening.<br /> Did you change the Guardians to better fit Ascension or did you push the themes more into an Awakening direction?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25507-nobby","name":"nobby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25507_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"66"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-21T18:27:23-08:00","id":1369481,"title":"","content":"A large part of it has to do with my Protocols, which are sort of analogous to the Traditions in Vampire, with the major globals ones being established by the Grand Council of the Nine Traditions (or in the case of Tradition-specific ones, by the leadership of that Tradition), and then moving downward from regional groups like the Islamic Web of Faith or Africa's Great House of Stone then to more local ones by city or regional Councils or even individual Chantries (or in some cases even specific rules a Cabal would establish for its members).<br /> One of my main ones was the Veil of Secrecy, which was established both to protect the Awakened members of the Traditions from outside enemies and to protect the wider mundane world from the more dangerous aspects of what the Awakened deal with. It's part and parcel with the Traditions effectively being a resistance group against a global tyrannical conspiracy; they have to protect themselves and their activities from discovery while also protecting innocent bystanders from being caught in the crossfire of their secret war.<br /> The former part involves shielding themselves from Technocratic discovery and infiltration, corruption by the Nephandi, attacks by witch hunters, and getting entangled in the plots of other supernaturals. The latter involves making sure the ultimate truths about Reality and the Tapestry are only revealed to those properly initiated (usually through the various Gateway Arts each Tradition - and even Craft - have to introduce members and even acolytes to their world view and beliefs), that dangerous Mystic items and magic don't cause unnecessary harm to the unawakened masses that one dwells around, and that the invisible world of spirits, demons, and other beings don't cause damage to the material world.<br /> So, my Ascension Guardians of the Veil basically serve as the Nine Traditions' intelligence service, police force, and neighborhood watch. Various subgroups within it focus on infiltrating the Technocracy, keeping Talismans or cursed objects out of mundane hands, shutting down cults devoted to Infernalism, Spectres or worse, protecting the families and friends of Mages from being targeted by the Technocracy, investigating crimes within the Awakened community, shooing nosey bystanders away from Chantries or Nodes, and so forth.<br /> Basically, life the other three groups (Adamantine Arrow, Mysterium, Silver Ladder), I ended up incorporating them as something for when the game would grow beyond the local level and start getting into a more global scale, be it in the realm of military action, scholarly research, politics, or intrigue/espionage. Likewise, I incorporated the Free Council as a way for Orphan, outsider, and Hollow One centric chronicles to move up a level into regional or even global activities within the Ascension War.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T07:04:35-08:00","id":1369571,"title":"","content":"I've powered up the number of Crafts and Camps, included the Arcana system of MtAw2nd, and retooled the Tremere as more powerful antagonists. Nephandi and Mads are also way more careful and stealthy in their way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/822-daemonchrno","name":"DaemonChrno","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar822_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-22T16:45:53-08:00","id":1369694,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#1c2123\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">So, my Mage: The Dark Ages (as opposed to DA:M) set up. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1c2123\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">I prefer to set games in mid to late 12th century. This is mostly just because I prefer that period when Constantinople hasn't been sacked, the Third Crusade hasn't started, and Richard I, Eleanor of Aquitaine, Frederick Barbarossa, William the Lion, and Alfonso VIII are active. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1c2123\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">I also have the Tremere outed as vampires already, and the Order of Hermes having begun a crusade/wizards' march against them. This is a shadow war, but other Mages and Vampires are already getting caught up in it as both collateral damage and from blowback by other forces. Those aware of this fight have started referring to it as &quot;the Sorcerers' Crusade&quot;. One of the forces causing this blowback is the Sword of Gabriel, also known as the Sacred Order of Pure Thought and True Faith. This conflict has caused the various factions of Awakened - known as Fellowships - to tentatively ally with each other when their needs seem to align. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1c2123\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">The period Essences are based on the four classic elements, Fire (Dynamic), Earth (Pattern), Water (Primordial), and Air (Questing). The Spheres are Connection (Correspondence), Fate, Energia (Forces), Nature (Life), Materia (Matter), Soul (Mind), Creation (Prime), Celestia (Spirit), and Eternity (Time). </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1c2123\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">My Fellowships: </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1c2123\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">The <b>Craftmasons</b>, <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">proto-Humanist artisans, artificers, and scholars formed during the Carolingian Renaissance by remnants of the Brotherhood of the Rule. (And no, they are not in any way tied to the Order of Hermes, whom they detest.) They seek to perfect God's fallen creation and help usher in a new golden age for mankind, using faith and reason to advance the lot of the common man and serve the common good. Medicine, trade, and sacred architecture all serve this end, as does education and the destruction of monsters and other demonic forces that prey on humanity and sew disorder. They work their arts through sacred mathematics and geometry, especially in the design of buildings and landscapes, as well as through alchemy, master crafts, and forceful logical argument. They focus on Connection, Creation (Prime), and Matereria (Matter).<br /> The Craftmasons include several specialist Guilds, each with its own focus and concerns:</div></div> </div> </div></span></span><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><ul><li><i>The Bright Lions</i>, alchemists and natural philosophers seeking to unlock the secrets of creation so as to perfect it and themselves. Their knowledge of herbs and poisons make them skilled healers and assassins, while their alchemical formula are able to produce elixirs and other objects of wonder.</li> <li><i>The Cosians</i>, a collective of traders, merchants, healers, and diplomats, they originated out of the wool and cloth trades and are primarily made up of and led by women. They view themselves as the menders of a broken world and weavers of a better future, with a sacred calling to mend damages and bind together communities.</li> <li><i>The Iron Maul</i>, warriors and hunters sword to use the tools of reason and faith to defend the helpless and destroy the enemies of humanity. Members wield masterpiece weapons of war and alchemical objects of destruction in their never-ending vigil.</li> <li><i>The Golden Pyramid</i>, an alliance of merchants, traders, and guild masters who help fund the works of the greater fellowship. To them, wealth and commerce are tools that improve society and uplift the individual to greatness. They prefer subtle arts of diplomacy and manipulation blended with the best tools and tricks good coin can purchase.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Vengeful monster hunter, Disgruntled nun wishing to become a surgeon, Journeyman artisan striving to craft his masterpiece, Waldensian rabble rouser, Alchemist assassin.)</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>The Gardeners of the Tree of Life</b>, <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">secret pagans who preserve the mystic arts and sacred rites of the Old Ways while acting as caretakers of the Tree of Life, that which connects and ties together every living thing in creation. Tracing themselves back by blood to the primordial Wyck, they continue to practice the secret rituals of the ancient Greeks, Germans and Celts. Through the use of herbs, chants, and sacrifices to forgotten gods, they help remind the world of its forgotten past and the power of Nature and the Wild. Most work their Art through the Old Ways of pre-Christian Europe, including herbalism and handcrafts, blood sacrifices, and pagan rituals. Others incorporate more mainstream methods such as astrology and folk charms. They focus on Energia (Forces), Fate, and Nature.<br /> The Gardeners include traditional Branches, each with its own practices and rites:<ul><li><i>The Kindly Ones</i>, secretive and aloof even from their fellow Gardeners, they are twisters of fate who punish those who transgress against ancient laws and customs. All are skilled in the arts of divination and cursing, and traditionally work in covens of three.</li> <li><i><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#1c2123\">The </span></span></i><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><i>Kings of the Grove</i>, scions of ancient bloodlines, many going back to ancient gods or to the Wyck themselves. They hold positions of power and influence in villages, towns and noble houses. </span></span></li> <li><i>The Oaken Shield</i>, an order of knights who are secretly sworn to protect and defend the sacred places of Nature and of the Old Faiths. They wield powerful martial magics and even ancient artifact weapons.</li> <li><i>The Wild Heart</i>, shapeshifters and skin-riders who spend as much time in the form of beasts as they do men. Each has a totem animal tied to her heart's blood. Many live as hermits or foresters in the wilderness, but others hide within the confines of civilization.</li> <li><i>The Poor Sisters of Eve</i>, a heretical order of nuns devoted to the Mother of All, distrusted by many within the Fellowship. They practice the arts of herbalism, midwifery, and healing, as well as secret fertility rites and charm-making.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Wise woman of the woods, Secret power behind the Fief, Wandering troubadour searching for ancient tales, Noblewoman who secretly runs with wolves, Conflicted nun trying to reconcile her faith and magic.)</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>The Keepers of the Sacred Fire</b>, <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">a secret society devoted to preserving and safeguarding the wisdom of the angels within God's Creation, including those found within the unconverted and the ancients of the world. A mix of clergy and layman, many of them are monastics, scholars, and cunning men and women who carefully collect esoteric lore, and have a reputation as exorcists and healers. The fellowship is believed to trace itself back to the Vestal temples of Ancient Rome, if not further back, having become Christian in the latter half of the Roman Empire. The Keepers are masters of theurgy and demonology, focusing their arts through rituals calling on the true names of angels, herbalism, and folk charms. They focus on Celestia, Nature, and Soul. <br /> The Keepers include several dedicated Lodges, each with its own sacred calling and specialization:<ul><li><i>The Brotherhood of the Fox</i>, wandering travelers, tinkers, and troubadours who lurk among the more disreputable areas of society, usually causing trouble wherever they go. Members argue that such disruption and change is necessary to prevent Creation from stagnating, but more than a few simply enjoy playing the role of trickster or fool.</li> <li><i>The Hearthkeepers</i>, preservers of hearth and home, and of community and culture. Many are women, serving as village wise women, household servants, or nuns, working to preserve the unique wisdom and magic of women, including the rites of forgotten religions and goddesses. They view themselves as sacred guardians and healers devoted to the protection of their families and people.</li> <li><i>The Librum Numinia</i>, a collective of scholars, philosophers, and artists dedicated to collecting the stories and lore of Christendom and beyond, scouring them for the hidden wisdom of the angels and insight into the will and plans of God. They consider all lore, no matter how heretical or even potentially blasphemous to be valuable in their quest.</li> <li><i>The Red Spear of Longinus</i>, a martial order of knights, lay warriors, and crusaders sworn to the recovery and protection of sacred relics, both Christian and otherwise, and to battle the machinations of demons and other unclean spirits.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Young peasant touched by angels, Demon-hunting knight, Cryptic hermit who speaks with animals, Traveling seller of questionable charms and relics, Divinely inspired artist.)</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 03-07-2020, 06:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-24T04:29:05-08:00","id":1370032,"title":"","content":"I am also considering a mash up game mixing Ascension and Awakening. I am actually working on two versions. One using Sorcerer's Crusade as the base, then adding on the magic system from Awakening. I have interesting ideas of how to mix Wisdom and Fortune/Scourge. The other idea is more mechanically along the lines of Awakening, using elements of M20, in a modern setting.<br /> As for the back story, I am sticking with oWoD. The Paths and Atlantis deal is a buzz kill. But I do like the Orders, and will incorporate them using ideas read here (much thanks to NOoC). <br /> One thing I want to do with metaplot, in both versions of my idea, is to put reality into flux. The game permits time travel, so the events of the 21st century can affect the past quite easily. Reality is warped as a result of the end of the Ascension war. No one won. Instead, everything gets reshaped so that there is no war. But that does not work out.Some people have distorted memories, a la Mandella effect.<br /> Full disclosure: I am not a big fan of the oWoD. I find it preferable to CoD, but really, I am only into Mage. There is no such thing as a &quot;good&quot; vampire, and werewolves are monsters. Not a fan of mixed games at all. But, this group I found big into this stuff, so I can adapt, and I *am* a huge Order of Hermes fan.<br /> Which leads me to the an alternate reality idea to use that will keep these guys interested and surprised. I want to use the Ars Magica version of the Orderof Hermes. House Tremere is intact and a cornerstone of the Order. No vampire Tremere or liche Tremere. Just the proto fascist magi everyone knows and loves.<br /> For those of you unfamiliar with Ars Magica, it does not at all work the way that Mage may lead you to believe. White Wolf published 3rd edition and did publist a lot of materia, said material formed the foundation for the backstory of Vampire 1 then in turn Mage 1. But since then, Atlas has spent over 20 years (and two more editions) trying to erase or reshape the WW influence. For example, the Craftmasons who destroyed Mistridge become incorporated into the Order as part of House Ex-Miscelanea (and now call themselves the Rusticani, craft magic magi). That is another surprise of the altered Mandella reality I want to throw at them.<br /> As for the other Traditions, not sure how to alter them. I do prefer the more modern names as presented in M20, so I will use those. In fact, one idea for the Council of Nine is to kick a few trads out and add a few crafts in to take their place. The Etherites and Adepts would instead be part of the modern Orderof Reason. Maybe the Order of Hermes is a separate group apart from the Council. So then you would have the Council, the OoR, the OoH, the Disparatae Alliance, and the Exocratic Union. Maybe you cam see where I am going with that. *They* are the ones responsible for the Mandella effect fluctuating reality. Then Banishers and Nephandi and Marauders and so on. Mix it all in a pot like gumbo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-14T17:27:47-07:00","id":1374837,"title":"","content":"My alternate Dark Ages Fellowships pt 2.<br /> <br /> <b>The Memento Mori</b>, a mystery cult devoted to keeping the secrets of life, death, and destiny, with roots among Ancient Greece and Rome, as well among the pagan north. Hiding among various monastic and knightly orders, merchant houses, and noble families, they see themselves as agents and facilitators of God's divine plan and the hands of fate. When the world goes against the divine plan, the world suffers, as do the people in it. Members of the fellowship are as much healers and diplomats as they are killers and manipulators. They often work their Arts through old forms of necromancy and divination, often calling upon ancestor spirits, forgotten gods, and the darker angels for information and aid. Sacred oaths, backed up by the power of God and Fate, provide additional power. They also practice the sciences of alchemy and astrology, as well as the more common crafts of herbalism and physic. They focus on Celestia, Eternity, and Fate.<br /> The Memento Mori include a number of hidden circles, each with its own mysteries and rites.<ul><li><i>The Golden Chalice</i>, a secret society of courtiers, spies, and assassins originating among the courts of Constantinople. They mix alchemical arts with the powers of dark angels to subtly manipulate individuals and societies in to accordance with God's divine plan.</li> <li><i>The Knights of Nyssa</i>, an order of crusader knights sworn to the protection of sacred sites and relics, both Christian and Pagan. Famed for the power of their sacred oaths and blessed weapons, they specialize in the destruction of wicked ghosts and other baleful dead.</li> <li><i>The Leaden Coin</i>, a collective of merchant houses who trace their linages back to ancient families of power in the Mediterranean, Northern Europe, and the British Isles. They use the arts of divination and necromancy to make and grow great fortunes, funding the Fellowship and influencing society.</li> <li><i>The Rhymers</i>, wondering minstrels, troubadours, and bards wise in the ways of Fate. They offer advice, protection, and prodding to to those who need help in reaching their ordained destiny, preferring the subtle arts of musical manipulation, folk charms, and rhyming spells.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Traveling troubadour helping a young knight become a hero, Clever daughter of a wealthy merchant, Lost crusader haunted by his past, Faceless servant who sees all, Mistress of the arts of death.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Sacred Passion</b>, a heretical Gnostic-Christian sect based on a text called the Nine Sacred Passions, teaching that all emotions are a vital link between the human soul and the divine world. By pursing the passions of Joy, Love, Sympathy, Ambition, Grief, Fear, Envy, Hate, and Rage, one is able to surpass the limits of the material world and hear the heartbeat of God. The Fellowship has attracted an eclectic collection of nobles, clergy, artisans, monastics, and commoners, all of whom pursue their passions through prayer and meditation, ritual magic rites, alchemical and herbalist concoctions, theurgic summoning of angels and demons, and fasting and other ritual penance. The more powerful the emotional response, the better a focus for their Arts. They prefer to focus on Eternity, Nature, and Soul.<br /> The Sacred Passion includes several Cults, each with its own methods and focus.<ul><li><i>L'Jongleur Amore</i>, focused on Love and Joy, striving to bring beauty and romance into the fallen world through chivalry and courtly love. They are nobles, knights, troubadours, and poets who excel at the subtle social graces of Ars Cupidae. (Rumors persist of a rival faction devoted to Lust and Jealousy who work to cultivate pain and tragedy.)</li> <li><i>The Silver Ladder</i>, focused on Ambition and Envy, striving for power and influence in both the mundane and mystical realms. Nobles, clergy, and merchants devoted to self-interest and personal advancement through the use of social manipulation, favors wrested from angels or demons, and alchemical elixirs (including poisons).</li> <li><i>The </i><i>Studiosi</i>, focused on Hate and Rage, an underground movement of radical Gnostic heretics devoted to the destruction of the Church Material that serves the Rex Mundi and of the very prisons of their own flesh through self-denial, flagellation, and violence.</li> <li><i>The </i><i>Weeping Eye</i>, focused on Sympathy and Grief, who believe they have a sacred duty to bear witness to the pain and suffering of the world. Largely a monastic sect, they draw strength and wisdom from seeing how others deal with misfortune and loss.</li> </ul>(Concepts: L'Belle Dame sans Merci, Priest experiencing sin so that he may repent, Itinerant healer overwhelmed by others' pain, Violent Cathar radical, Matchmaking noblewoman unable to find her own true love.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-15T11:30:04-07:00","id":1374945,"title":"","content":"My game is set in the years leading up to WWII and I'm focusing on the coming conflict, so I've made quite a few changes to both Traditions and the Technocracy. <br /> <br /> The Technocracy has split into three different factions. There's the Union and two Special Projects Divisions, one for the USSR (the Technocratic Soviets) and another for the Third Reich (<i>Projektabteilung Thorwald</i>). The Elemental Dragons of Asia are largely their own masters.<br /> <br /> I've added another faction to the Society of Ether, the Free State of Ether, based in an Umbral Realm resembling the South Pacific. This was the staging area for Czar Vargo, and his ideals are very much dominant for these Etherites. There's also a splinter group who left the Society and joined the Technocratic Soviets.<br /> <br /> The Third Reich is home to a conspiracy of Technocrats, Traditionalists, vampires, and Garou. They operate under the Black Sun banner and are led by a secret cabal of powerful supernaturals. In the game, the Black Sun organization has fielded a mixed cabal with a Hermetic, an It X in a hardsuit, and a Get of Fenris, supported by Extraordinary Citicens and teleported in by yet another mixed cabal.<br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes has become divided between two parties, the conservative Party of the High Tower and the proto-fascist Goethean Party. This division has divided houses and chantries, but the majority of the European chantries are controlled by the Goetheans. If also added another house, the Lictori, under Ex Misc that basically act as muscle and secret police for the Goetheans.<br /> <br /> In Japan, the Elemental Dragons have all been subjugated by the Iron Chrysanthemum, an offshoot of the Earth Dragons.<br /> <br /> The night-folk of both the Third Reich and the Japanese Empire are secretly led by a group of Nephandi, serving a European Earthbound and the Yama King Mikaboshi, respectively.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Any technology distinguishable from magic is insufficiently advanced.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12101-nitromidas","name":"Nitromidas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12101_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-16T20:40:59-07:00","id":1375217,"title":"","content":"And part 3.<br /> <br /> <b>The Subtle Ones</b>, a mysterious secret society devoted to the unity of all Creation under G-d's holy design, and to working to understand and further that plan. The Fellowship is embedded in universities and other places of learning, as well as merchant houses, noble courts, and the clergy. They also have a number of members from Europe's Jewish populations. All of them share the twin traits of education and discretion, and in their quest for unity between all persons and the divine spirit of G-d, they play games of politics and influence, with moves and plots that can take years, if not lifetimes, to play out. The Subtle Ones work their Arts through prayer, meditation, and activities that focus the mind, including mathematics, geometry, logic, and music. They also study alchemy and astronomy, as well as debate, symbolism, and theurgy, and are able to defend themselves through stealth and assassination. They focus on Connection, Fate, and Soul.<br /> The Fellowship includes several distinct Crafts, each with its own path to unity.<ul><li><i>The Holy Mountain</i>, masons, architects, and engineers who construct churches, castles, and other buildings to strengthen society and bind communities together, as well as strengthen the ties between man and G-d with pilgrimage and trade.</li> <li><i>The Invisible Blade</i>, warriors, assassins, and agents provocateur moving without a trace through Medieval society, removing enemies of the Fellowship and their goal of unity by whatever means necessary.</li> <li><i>\u200bThe Silk Road</i>, members of merchant houses, trade ships, and fairs who work to encourage the development of financial relations across Medieval Europe and beyond, specializing in social Arts and alchemy.</li> <li><i>The University of Light</i>, alchemists, astronomers, mathematicians and musicians studying the nature and secrets of G-d's Creation in order to better understand it and to refine their own souls. Ultimately they hope to unify with angels and become one with G-d.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Court astronomer, Friendly merchant offering goods from a dozen kingdoms, Quiet lady of the court who specializes in character assassination, Journeyman mason who dreams of building the greatest cathedral on earth, Secret guardian of the Jewish quarter.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Voices of Mercy</b>, a heretical Christian sect drawing on Gnostic and even ancient pagan beliefs, believing in the all powerful One True God from whom all Creation originates. All mankind longs to return to the One True God and be reunited in harmony with His righteousness and mercy. To this end, the Voices spread the sacred song of the One, guiding people back to him, ministering to those in need, aiding the lost, and defending the innocent from the Darkness. Scattered across Medieval Europe, they include bishops, crusaders, monks, mystics, and laymen, all of whom will redeem the world or die trying. The work their Arts through prayer, song, and other forms of speech, augmented with holy rituals, sacred relics, theurgy, and mystic visions. They focus on Creation, Energia, and Soul. <br /> The Voices include several Choirs, each with its own philosophies and callings.<ul><li><i>The Knights of Redemption</i>, founded by veterans of the First Crusade influenced by certain Gnostic heresies of the Holy Land, believing that all souls are tied to the One True God, and therefore even the most fallen must be lead back to the light and redeemed so as to reunify Creation with the Divine.</li> <li><i>The Librum Sanctum</i>, scholars and philosophers who do everything possible to preserve and study sacred writings, relics and other treasures of faith, including the works of the ancient world, all of which provide insight into the One True God and His Creation.</li> <li><i>The Sisters of Gabrielle</i>, a secretive order of nuns who believe that the innocent must be protected from the Darkness, both spiritually and physically, Skilled healers, herbalists, and exorcists, many are also secretly trained in more martial arts.</li> <li><i>The White Doves</i>, with the sacred calling to spread the message of the mercy and succor of the One True God, they serve the Fellowship as messengers, missionaries, apologists, and when necessary investigators and spies.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Righteous crusader of mercy and redemption, Eternally questioning philosopher, Itinerant faith healer, Servant of the local bishop, Wise hermit of the forest.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T03:59:44-07:00","id":1375249,"title":"","content":"In my game, the Technocracy are experimenting with ramping up the amount of humans being born / transported to colonies on the Horizon and beyond. But now they're facing the very real threat of growing Extraordinary human populations that have no connection to earth that now want to reunite with their terrestrial ancestors...aided by a few elements in each Convention.<br /> <br /> The NWO and Syndicate Media Control are trying to introduce an entirely new sphere of media culture for the these ballooning populations to be distracted by whilst simultaneously making sure there's no rebellions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26024-distant-choir","name":"Distant Choir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26024_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T11:43:31-07:00","id":1375307,"title":"","content":"If anyone wants to have a closer look at my setting, I've got this <a href=\"https://radio-noir.fandom.com/wiki/Radio_Noir_Wiki\" target=\"_blank\">Wiki</a>. As this game is going, I'm holding back on what I've made public, but you should be able to get a feel for the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Any technology distinguishable from magic is insufficiently advanced.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12101-nitromidas","name":"Nitromidas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12101_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-17T15:51:43-07:00","id":1375329,"title":"","content":"These are all really interesting stuff and I'm always fascinated to see the different methods that people have for all the various sorts of crafts that must exist in the setting of Mage and different ways of handling stuff like the Technocracy.<br /> <br /> The idea of the technocratic colonies is also something definitely interesting that I can see being really fun to play with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-18T16:21:59-07:00","id":1375511,"title":"","content":"And my two Dark Ages antagonist sects (and some lesser groups). <br /> <br /> <b>The Sword of Gabriel</b>, aka The Sacred Order of Pure Thought and True Faith. Founded on the divine revelation of One World, One God, One Church, they swear to uphold and protect the Church of Rome, to seek out and eliminate heresy, to convert unbelievers, and to defend the faithful from darkness and error. Those priests, crusaders, scholars, nobles, and laymen, known as the Sword-sworn, work their Arts through prayer, church ritual, holy relics, the true names of angels, and the power of God's Word. Healing and divinely revealed wisdom are just as important as God's wrath, but in the wake of their Illuminated Inquisition called to reign in the excesses of wizards and witches, many are starting to favor blade and fire over correction and conversion. They focus on Celestia, Energia, and Soul. <br /> The Sword has a number of organized Cabals, each with its own views and methods of caring out their sacred mission.<ul><li><i>The White Tower </i>: The World of God is a sacred thing, as is His Creation and the sacred revelations he has left for the faithful. Scholars, philosophers, and other learned men and women, they collect and study knowledge and lore, ensuring it is in agreement with the teachings of the Church before allowing it to be passed on to the masses.</li> <li><i>The Order of the Lion </i>: Christ will return not as a lamb, but as a lion, and the members of the Order are sworn to be lions of God. Steadfast in devotion and relentless in the pursuit of evil, these crusader knights and lay men at arms, as well as those who sponsor them, bear arms in service to the Sacred Order and the Church.</li> <li><i>The Holy Banner </i>: The Word of God and the reign of the Holy Church must be spread to all corners of the world. Heathen and heretic must be converted, while the lords of Christendom must be made to serve the ends of the Church. Through use of law, politics, finance, and conversion, these prelates, merchants, ladies in waiting, and seneschals all carry the Banner.</li> <li><i>The Razors </i>: Sin and error are a sickness which must be cut out of the body of the faithful. To this end exist the secretive members of the Razors, walking invisibly through all corners of medieval society, even among Jews and other nonbelievers. Much of their time is spent gathering information and identifying potential threats or allies, but when called upon they also act as holy assassins.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Faithful lady in waiting, Fearless demon hunter, Stern abbotess with no patience for frivolity, University metaphysicist, Vengeful victim of magic misuse.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Pax </b><b>Hermetica</b>, aka the Order of Hermes. Considering themselves the only real practitioners of the True Art and all other practitioners and fellowships beneath them. Already having made no friends with their overbearing behavior toward the various Fellowships, their recent shadow war against the undead has brought nothing but misery and hardship to the mages of Europe. Save the lost Diedne and the now condemned Tremere, all of the founding Houses still exist, as well as the new Ex Miscellanea. <br /> <br /> (Concepts: Relentless vampire hunter, Lord of the Deep Woods, Wandering trouble maker, Journeyman rune smith, Wealthy merchant prince, Rootless explorer seeking new wisdom, Half-mad mystic seer, Genius far ahead of her time, Arcane assassin.)<br /> <br /> <i>Artificers</i>, aka Brotherhood of the Rule (which I'm not even going to try to translate into Byzantine Greek), eastern counterparts to the Craftmasons, they consider themselves the true inheritors of the ancient Roman Ars Praeclarus. They work in a much smaller scale than their &quot;backward&quot; Western cousins, creating wondrous automatons, ingenious mechanical devices, and alchemical marvels. <br /> <br /> <i>The Storm-Crows</i>, a catch all term for number of mages who owe allegiance to no fellowship. They are a disturbing new phenomenon brought about by the upheavals of both the Pax Hermetica's overzealous crusade and the Sword-sworn's excessive retribution, as a number of long standing covens, chantries, and schools are destroyed and newer mages have no one to teach them properly. <br /> <br /> <i>The Web of Faith</i> (Tahaluf al'Muminin?), the dominant alliance of magi in the Islamic world, from Iberia to the Levant and beyond. They are largely a mystery to European mages, but include the House of the Crescent Moon, the Darwishim, the Muktashfu al Daw' (Discoverers of Light), the Hadiqat al Salam (Garden of Peace), and others. <br /> <br /> Those who are well read or traveled claim there are further alliances beyond the Islamic lands in Ethiopia, India, and China.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-22T15:40:35-07:00","id":1376163,"title":"","content":"My own take on the Technocracy is slightly different than canon, drawing a lot on 1st edition presentation and real world technocratic progressive statism, the conspiracy genre, and some of the darker implications of some of the ways magic can be uses by unfriendly conspiracies. <br /> <br /> The Technocracy is <b>NOT</b> based on or devoted to science and technological advancement; They are devoted to control and conformity of the masses. Science and technology are just tools to further this end, the same way religion used to be back in the old days. The Technocracy seeks to remake humanity and human society into something easy to control and shape in their preferred image, a Utopian society that places themselves firmly in charge and the masses as obedient sheep. The Traditions, with their belief in individualism and personal ascension, are a threat to this plan. These are basically the bad guys you've seen in things like The X-Files, 1984, Brave New World, The Manchurian Candidate, and The Trial. <br /> Where my Mages have Gnosis (or Arete if you prefer), representing one's understanding of the greater secrets of Reality and Magic, Technocrats have Conditioning, representing their level of indoctrination and training in the Technocratic mindset and world view. And where my Mages have Wisdom as a morality stat, Technocrats have Obedience. And where Mages work toward Ascension as they increase in Gnosis (and Nephandi fall downwards), Technocrats go off on a tangent that makes them not human anymore. (And the details of which differ for each Convention.)<br /> Low level Technocrats are usually recruited/scouted and initiated the same way Mages are, often appealing to their better natures of building a better world, advancing science, etc. It's only later as they go deeper down the rabbit hole that they become more and more aware of (and perhaps numb to) the darker parts of the Technocracy. The best hope for a Technocrat is to eventually realize that they are on the wrong side and end up defecting, usually to the Sons of Ether or the Virtual Adepts, but sometimes other groups. <br /> (The Technocracy uses Sciences instead of Spheres. Spacial Science, Probability Science, Energy Science, Biological Science, Material Science, Psychological Science, Quantum Science, Dimensional Science, and Temporal Science.)<br /> Historically, while there was an Order of Reason founded in the late Medieval period, it had largely fallen apart by end of the 18th century and the French Revolution and Napoleonic Wars were its death knell. The new Enlightened Society of Order and Reason was formed in 1851 in London. After the disruptions of WW2, they reorganized into the Enlightened Union of Technocratic Order, more commonly known as the Technocratic Union or Technocracy. This also began a series of pogroms against the Traditions and other groups, which coincided with the Virtual Adepts joining the Traditions. <br /> (The Sons of Ether and Virtual Adepts have never been part of my Technocracy, instead being formed by those devoted to the free and open pursuit of scientific discovery and the exchange of information. So it's always been a cabal of five, even back with the Order of Reason.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-22T17:01:18-07:00","id":1376177,"title":"","content":"<b>The New World Order</b><br /> <br /> <i>We do disagreeable things so that ordinary people here and elsewhere can sleep safely in their beds at night. Is that too romantic?Of course, we occasionally do very wicked things.</i><br /> - John LeCarre, The Spy Who Came in From the Cold<br /> <br /> The ultimately origins of the NWO begin with the Revelation of Pure Thought of God as Revealed by His Messenger Gabriel Archangel to Claudius Deditactus during the reign of Constantine the Great in 320AD. This dictate of One World, One God, One Faith and its idea of all humanity united under a single mono-faith empire was upheld and advanced by the <i>Sacred Order of Pure Thought and True Faith</i> through their infiltration of the Roman Catholic Church. However, the first major break among the faithful came with the rise of Islam and the breakaway sect known as the <i>Righteous Brethren of Purity and Submission</i>. While original Sacred Order has long since faded into history, the Righteous Brethren still exist in the Middle East as a persistent antagonist of the Web of Faith and the Ahl I Batin. <br /> With the Dark Ages as the Order of Hermes starting their crusades against the undead, the Sacred Order started their own Illuminated Inquisition (not to be confused with the mundane Papal Inquisition or the Society of Leopold's Shadow Inquisition) against not only the Hermetics, but the heretical Voices of Mercy and various pagan sects. Eventually they allied with the Craftmasons and three other like-minded factions to form the Order of Reason in the 14th century. All of this soon lead to the Burning of Baerwald in Germany and Wyndgarde's March through Britain, as well as the betrayal of the Knights Templar. <br /> The Reformation caused a schism within the Sacred Order, with a majority remaining within the Catholic Church and taking part in the Counter Reformation, but some taking up the banner of Protestant faiths. While the Anglican and Lutheran Orders ties with various crowns allowed them to carry on as normal, other sects found themselves hard pressed by their former fellows. Ironically, these groups would end up finding sanctuary among their former enemies of the Voices of Mercy. <br /> By the time of the 17th and 18th century, British, French, German and other factions managed to reorganize themselves into the <i>Bringers of Order and Light</i>, blending together the new ideals of reason and the Enlightenment with that of a powerful earthly government and the Divine Right of Kings. Natural philosophy and logic became powerful weapons against witches, demons and heresy. In the wake of the French Revolution and the Napoleonic Wars, these Lightbringers became one of the founders of the new Enlightened Society of Reason. <br /> The 19th and 20th centuries saw the rise of powerful national governments, including the belief in the unquestioned leadership of educated elites with the proper ideas and credentials and its natural expressions in Socialism, Bureaucratism, Communism, Corporatism, and Fascism. After WW2, the Lightbringers again reorganized into the modern <i>New World Order</i>. Under this guise, they carry out the Technocracy's pogroms against its enemies and advance their ultimate goal of One World, One Government, One Mind through the tools of social conformity, orthodoxy, and peer pressure, propaganda and information control, public education and mass media, public surveillance and data gathering, social engineering, psychological warfare, and indoctrination. <br /> The modern Order has three methodologies. <i>The Ivory Tower</i> work in the fields of record collection, propaganda, reeducation, and information sanitation, ultimately deciding what the masses should be exposed to and what opinions they should be having. <i>The Panopticon</i> work in surveillance, data collection, internal security and threat detection, constantly watching the masses for signs of deviation and independence. And <i>The Agency</i> work in field operations, disinformation, external security and assassination, going out among the masses to deal directly with threats to the Order. <br /> Among their forces are the Men in Black (faceless field agents who can pose as government forces and carry high tech gear), Greys (advanced agents able to blend invisibly into society to carry out their missions), Whites (aka Whitewashers, advanced agents who liaise with other Conventions, acting as spokespersons and troubleshooters who seem to never get their hands dirty), Big Brothers/Sisters (elite internal security specialists and loyalty enforcers), Sympathizers (interrogation specialists), Snowmen (liaisons with the mundane public who specialize in spin doctoring, disinformation and illusions), Re-educators (specialists in mindscaping, brainwashing, and reprograming), Censors (specialists in scrubbing unwanted date from public view and the minds of others), Gatekeepers (security specialists who restrict access to classified and unhelpful information), Collectors (data analysts who obsessively specialize in specific subjects), Talking Heads (mundane spokespersons to the masses disseminating the proper party line and talking points), Manchurians (brainwashed sleeper agents - including Mages - unaware of their conditioning until activated), and Watchers (engineered drones in the form of humans, animals, machines and other objects whose sensory input is recorded). <br /> NWO strongholds are usually referred to as Academies, Media Centers, or Safehouses, depending on the methodology in charge. In addition, the have Re-education Camps and, worst of all, Memory Holes. <br /> The ultimate end of NWO Conditioning is <i>The Hive Mind</i>. As you go above level 3 or 4, your mind becomes less and less your own. Eventually, you become just a mouth piece for a single collective consciousness with no real will or personality of your own. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-22T19:17:08-07:00","id":1376196,"title":"","content":"<b>Iteration X</b><br /> <br /> <i>It can't be bargained with. It can't be reasoned with. It can't feel pity, or remorse, or fear. And it will not stop, ever, until you are dead.</i><br /> - Kyle Reese, The Terminator (1984)<br /> <br /> In the ancient lands of Khem during the reign of Pharaoh Djoser (2630BC), the exalted Imhotep of Memphis transcribed the Sacred and Most Secret Scrolls of Ptah, revealing the cornerstones of creation, the sacred geometry of the universe, and architecture of the Eternal Monolith. His disciples became the <i>Brotherhood of the Rule</i>, devoted to the doctrine that any and everything within creation can be measured and quantified, and therefore controlled and perfected. Eventually, these teachings would reach the Roman Republic and Empire, where the teaching of Ars Praeclarus was codified by the <i>Collegium Praecepti</i>. <br /> By the Dark Ages, the doctrine diverged into three strains. The Collegium Praecepti continued within the lands of the Byzantines, while in the Muslim regions it inspired the <i>Muktashfu al Daw'</i> (Discoverers of Light). And within Charlemagne's Holy Roman Empire, Wulfgang Reisman founded the <i>Craftmasons</i>. While the Discoverers would join the Web of Faith, many of them eventually being largely subsumed by both the Ahl I Batin's University of Light and the Celestial Chorus's Alexandrian Society, the Craftmasons and Collegium would eventually reunify as one of the founders of the Order of Reason. (They would also see the defection to their ranks of a number of younger members of House Verditus.)<br /> \u200bThe dawn of the Industrial Revolution and early theories of Socialism led to a revolution within the ranks of the artificers as well. They reorganized themselves as the <i>International Brotherhood of Mechanists</i>, devoted to constructing a well-ordered and egalitarian industrial utopia. As part of this, they helped found the Enlightened Society of Reason in 1851, and developed a number of steam powered and electrical machines, clockwork biomechanics, and analytical engines. <br /> With the 20th century, the Mechanists devoted themselves fully to industrialized warfare, social planning, and brutal repression in the quest to create the New Man and Utopian State. When the Society was reorganized as the Technocratic Union after WW2, they also upgraded themselves into <i>Iteration X</i>. As such, they continue to work toward their technological utopia via the military-industrial complex, Information Age warfare, the merging of man with machine. They strive to perfect humanity and society through the use of strict social order, material humanism and atheism, collectivism, indoctrination, cybernetic conversion, dependency on advanced technology, and the all powerful State. <br /> Iteration X has three methodologies. <i>Biomechanics</i> seeks the perfection of the human form through the fusion of man and machine via cybernetics and bionics. <i>Management</i> seeks the perfection of human society through orderly regulation, efficiency, and advanced technology. <i>Statistics</i> seeks the perfection of reality itself through advanced mathematics, data quantification, and predictive sciences. <br /> Among their forces are Exterminators (cyborg assassins able to pass as humans), War Machines (high power robotic shock troops with heavy weapons), Fixers (damage control specialists trained in maintenance, field medicine, cover up, and assassination), Inspectors (relentless detectives, trouble shooters and secret police), Programers (instructors who re-educate citizens in the ways of conformity and obedience), Weathermen (specialists in probability analysis tasked with identifying and eliminating future problems), Librarians (walking information systems who collect and process all available data on assigned subjects), Cyphers (deep undercover agents with reprogrammable minds), Komrads (gangs of engineered faceless cannon fodder), Drones (synthetic automatons simulating living creatures to act as spies and assassins), and Black Ice (savage security programs which patrol Technocracy controlled areas of the Digital Web). <br /> Iteration X strongholds include Laboratories, Workshops, Factories, and Think Tanks, as well as Gulags, depending on their focus. <br /> The ultimate end of Iteration X Conditioning is <i>The Machine</i>. The longer you are processed, the more you become less of a person and more an unthinking cog in an emotionless and amoral system. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-23T14:34:45-07:00","id":1376311,"title":"","content":"<b>The Progenitors </b><br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\">We shall conquer and beat the Nations of the Earth into the dust and reforge them in our self wrought Image; the Final Society, a new humanity without weakness or mercy, hard and pure. Our descendants will walk the hillside of that future, innocent beneath the stars, with no more between them and their naked will than a wolf has. THEN there will be Gods in the Earth.</span></span></i><br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"> - SM Stirling, The Domination of the Draka</span></span><br /> <br /> In the Fourth Age of Man (the Grecian Bronze Age), the Golden Doctrine of Apollon Phoebus was given to his son Asklepios, revealing the secrets of disease and healing, animal husbandry, metamorphosis, and divination so that he could recreate the Golden Race of Kronus. His disciples and followers created the <i>Golden Circle of Asklepios</i>, and their experiments and activities created countless hybrid beasts of legend, as well as there greatest creation, the race of super soldiers known as the Myrmidon.<br /> While the Golden Circle faded away by the Roman Empire, their secrets persisted in the Dark Ages and into the Renaissance. It was this time that saw the birth of the <i>Solificati</i>, the Crowned Ones, devoted to the use of alchemy and other arts to achieve spiritual and physical perfection, including immortality. With the Convention of the White Tower in the 14th century, the Solificati saw a schism, with the majority joining the Order of Reason and a misguided minority trying and failing to ally with the Mystic Traditions. <br /> The end of the Enlightenment included the Promethean Plague, a flood of alchemically created artificial human-like monsters who ran amok. The already scattered and disorganized Solificati collapsed. In their place, at the 1851 creation of the Enlightened Society of Reason came the <i>Eugenitors</i>. Their focus was the improvement of the human race - indeed, the creation of a race of over-men - through the use of controlled breeding, public hygiene, nutrition, enforced sterilization, and experimental surgeries. <br /> With the reorganization into the Technocratic Union, they became the <i>Progenitors</i>. They embraced the new fields of genetics as well as the growing area of pharmacology. Their ideal is an organized and biologically perfect society of those engineered for their selected roles. To this end they advocate the use of genetic engineering, population control, utilitarian bioethics, chemical control of emotion and personality, and biological warfare. <br /> The Progenitors have three methodologies. <i>Genengineering</i> focuses on human perfection via internal methods, including genetic modification and surgery. <i>Pharmacopics</i> focuses on human perfection via external methods, including drugs and nutrition. And <i>Purification</i> focuses on human perfection via social methods, including sanitation and doctrine. <br /> Among their forces are Alphas (genetically modified over-men with enhanced strength, intellect and health; the Convention's leadership), Betas (genetically modified assistants and aides designed for competence, obedience, and loyalty to the Greater Good), Delphians (experimental psychic diviners created through genetic engineering and designer drugs), Omegas (mindlessly obedient vat-grown clones used for simple labor and as cannon fodder), Watchdogs (designer animal security agents with heightened intelligence and loyalty), Tracers (bioenhanced hunters, both animal and humans, with enhanced senses and predatory instincts), Vectors (specialists in the distribution of biological agents, drugs, and propaganda to the masses), Lures (recruitment specialists designed for physical attractiveness, social grace, and pheromone production), Sweeps (telepathic security agents used for interrogation, counter intelligence, and loyalty enforcement), Sanitizers (clean up specialists trained in hazmat safety, quarantine procedures, cover ups, monster hunting, and assassination), Victors (super soldiers created through a mix of breeding, gene therapies, and drugs, equipped with advanced weapons), and Bio Beasts (vat grown monsters mixing multiple lifeforms used as shock troops and terror weapons). <br /> Progenitor strongholds include Laboratories, Factories, Farms, Theaters, Arks, and Hives.<br /> The ultimate end of Progenitor indoctrination and improvements is <i>The Master Race</i>. They are no longer human, and look upon the masses the way humans do cattle and other livestock. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-23T15:28:45-07:00","id":1376319,"title":"","content":"I've got to say that while I prefer a more nuanced and playable Technocracy, I would gladly implement what you're describing here as a revised and expanded Threat Null. Granted, the NWO and Syndicate are intrinsically tied to human society, and conceptually don't fare well when recast as a threat from beyond the veil. Threat Null's Agents and Residents felt\u2026 out of place as presented.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-23T16:49:45-07:00","id":1376326,"title":"","content":"It's definitely something that depends on the chronicle, as always with interpretations of either the Council of Traditions or Technocratic Union, but these are also most definitely extremely interesting and stuff I'd probably take inspiration from all sorts of different styles of chronicle. Awesome work NOOQ!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-23T17:31:58-07:00","id":1376337,"title":"","content":"My takes on the Syndicate and Void Engineers, which I will try to put up tomorrow, diverge even more from canon. But there is a recurring idea that, while the Technocracy is made up of humans and reflects some of the worst of humanity's totalitarian impulses, <i>something </i>was out there handing down these revelations, possibly for unfriendly purposes. (And it is entirely possible in this set up that the Ahl i Batin could have been the result of a sixth, maybe one that failed or was rogue from the others.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-23T17:54:58-07:00","id":1376341,"title":"","content":"Also, one of the side effects of my making the Solificati the medieval and renaissance era Progenitors<sup>1</sup> is that the modern Children of Knowledge Craft have a much stronger focus on Life, to the point I decided to make it their foundation Sphere, having to first learn to understand their own bodies and how chemicals and other agents effect them before they can start the quest for personal refinement.<br /> <br /> And, combined with other tweeks I made, I ended up with my nine main Crafts mirroring the Traditions with each having a different foundation Sphere. Batini (Connection), Wu Keng (Fate), Templars (Forces), CoK (Life), Taftini (Matter), Lions of Zion (Mind), Wu Lung (Prime), Bataa (Spirit), and Ngoma (Time).<br /> <br /> <sup>1</sup>After spending 3 or 4 years mulling it over, I realized the reason I never really liked the Hippocratic Circle is that they always felt like something built off of <i>what</i> the modern Progenitors did rather than <i>why</i> they do it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-24T19:11:53-07:00","id":1376469,"title":"","content":"<b>The Syndicate</b><br /> <br /> So, this will require a little bit of a prelude to explain a few things. <br /> First, while there is a tendency to associate the Syndicate with the idea of capitalism and its alleged evils or shortcomings, this really isn't the case. What people are thinking about when they mention these things is called Crony Capitalism, in which the business class and political class collude with each other in the form of regulations, corporate welfare, and other forms of state intervention (usually in return for large campaign donations, positions on boards after leaving government service, and other &quot;gifts&quot;) to stifle competition, corner markets, and get around the laws that apply to &quot;the little people&quot;. At the least invasive end of the spectrum are things like trade associations or set asides for preferred groups, but at the other end are countries where it's impossible to do any kind of business without taking on high ranking government officials as business partners or even majority owners (China is pretty notorious for this). <br /> Second, when I started looking at how the Syndicate operate, I decided that it was almost certain that they've always gotten ahead by cheating. They game the system, not just with money, but with Magic. And that means that, in addition to the usual sort of mesmerism and other aspects of Ars Cupidae you see with the High Guild in the Swashbucklers' Handbook, they can also directly manipulate Fate (which I use in place of Entropy), both their own and others. In fact, they can directly steal the good fortune and future potential of other people. But as part of their MO, they offer people money and other favors, but if they can't pay the Syndicate back, then they lose what they put up as collateral - their potential for the future. The members of the Syndicate take this potential for future success and use it for their own gain. (This is one of those instances where, like in the M:SC Order of Reason sourcebook, there is a difference between the lower ranks who are using &quot;advanced science&quot; Procedures/Rotes and the upper levels who are perfectly aware that they are altering Reality with abilities mundane people can never possess.)<br /> And just to note, part of this concept came from an old Twilight Zone episode, &quot;Person or Persons Unknown&quot;, but last year I came across an anime called <b>C:The Money of Soul and Possibility Control</b> that perfectly exemplified what I was trying to do with this. I really recommend it. <br /> <br /> And with that ... <br /> <br /> <i>And, for an instant, she stared directly into those soft blue eyes and knew, with an instinctive mammalian certainty, that the exceedingly rich were no longer even remotely human.</i> <br /> - William Gibson, Zero History<br /> <br /> During the Roman Republic, there was a mystery cult, <i>The Fraternal Order of Plutus</i>, devoted to a spirit known as the Pratonus Plouton. Members of the cult achieved great wealth as patrons, censors, and publicans, both through their personal connections, the fortune granted by their god, and the use of the arts of divination and necromancy. They used their money and influence to create an orderly society which was overseen by and primarily benefited the wealthy elite. <br /> The rise of Christianity and the fall of Rome let do the dissolution of the cult as a whole, but its traditions continued to be passed down, often with their spiritual patrons held to be angels of the material world. While many would have associations with the Hermetic House of Jerbiton, they constantly refused any greater involvement with the Order of Hermes, finding the medieval Craftmasons more in line with their thinking. By the High Middle Ages, the various inheritors of the cult had reincorporated themselves as the <i>Grand Guild of the Golden Pyramid</i>, involved in Europe's great trading cities and networks. It was here that they helped found the Order of Reason, and would be one of its strongest and most consistent pillars throughout the Renaissance and Enlightenment. They eagerly financed the Age of Discovery and the pillage of the New World, encouraged the creation of royal monopolies such as the British and Dutch East India Companies, and profited from the use of indentured servitude and slavery. <br /> In 1851, with the formation of the Enlightened Society of Reason, leaders of the Guild reincorporated themselves as the <i>Invisible Exchequer</i>. As captains of industry and champions of imperialism, they enjoyed an era of monopolies and a growing regulatory state's power to crush their competition. After the World Wars and Great Depression, with its war profiteering and command &amp; control economies, they joined the other Conventions in updating themselves as the <i>Syndicate</i>. They thrive in the modern world of crony capitalism, insider trading, market manipulation, and a moneyed elite who are clearly above the law. Their nigh-inconceivable levels of wealth allow them to headhunt and groom talent, equip themselves with the most bleeding edge of technology, and buy the hearts and minds of the masses. <br /> The Syndicate has three methodologies. <i>Investments</i> advance the interests of the elite through resource management, recruitment, patronage, training and indoctrination. <i>Adjustments</i> advance the interests of the elite through diplomacy and negotiation, spin doctoring, blackmail, assassination, and the direct manipulation of Fate. <i>Special Projects</i> advance the interests of the elite through security, debt collection, espionage, experimental research, and the repossession of forfeited collateral/futures. <br /> Among their forces are Assets (mundane people in debt to the Syndicate paying their way with service and favors), Patrons (managers of mundane assets and investments, cultivating people, places and projects for potential future profit), Providers (specialists in logistics and the management and distribution of equipment, funding and information), Boosters (elite Providers trained in the collection, manipulation, and distribution of Quintessence), Visionaries (specialists trained in probability analysis, event forecasting, and strategic planning), Salesmen (field agents trained in the arts of desire and diplomacy, handling recruitment, indoctrination and propaganda), Spin Doctors (elite Salesmen trained indirect fortune manipulation and mental operations), Headhunters (field agents trained for troubleshooting, tracking, kidnapping and assassination), Spectres (field agents trained in espionage, infiltration, and undercover work), White Knights (elite combat units used as security and strike teams, with the best equipment and training money can buy), Repo Men (elite agents who specialize in debt collection through the use of organ harvesting, life erasure, and removal of potential/future and even self), and Hollow Men (Assets who have defaulted on their debts, their entire future and even sense of self removed to serve as disposable servants and canon fodder). <br /> Syndicate strongholds include Offices, Tradehouses, Showrooms, Vaults, and Retreats.<br /> The ultimate end of Syndicate indoctrination, privilege and adjustments is to become one of The Elite, far above the common masses and their petty concerns or emotions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-24T21:14:01-07:00","id":1376481,"title":"","content":"I'm assuming that in your setup, there's no such thing as Primal Utility? You seem to be handling everything value-related through Fate. (Not something I object to; I've been developing a Path for Mage: the Awakening that was inspired by the Syndicate, and has Fate and Space as its Ruling Arcana. So I'm well-versed in the notion of Fate in the context of \u201cmoney magic\u201d.) It's just that I can see roles for Fate, Correspondence, <i>and</i> Prime in the Syndicate's paradigm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-25T18:13:22-07:00","id":1376636,"title":"","content":"Primal Utility would probably be a specialty of Quantum Science based on human perception and the Observer Effect as it applies to the values people put on people, places, and objects actually causing changes to Reality on a quantum (ie magical) level. And it would be part of what the Investments methodology handle, and what the Boosters are specifically trained in.<br /> I tend to have Fate focus on the ideas of probability, fortune, and destiny. If you default to this version of the Syndicate, they repossess whatever good things they gave you (money, fame, fortune, a political career, your spouse and kids), but seize whatever future destiny you might've had that you put up as collateral for the &quot;loan&quot;.<br /> Since I adapted Awakening's Sphere set up (Knowing, Compelling, Ruling, etc.), they can do a fair amount of stuff with just levels 1 - 3 (basic manipulation of probability, manipulate the strengths and weaknesses of people and objects, create chains of interactions between multiple targets), but at level 4 and 5, they can radically alter or distort how things or circumstances grow or change and completely create or destroy someone's destiny.<br /> While Probability Science/Fate is their primary Science (with the ability to recognize potential winners and losers and start making sure that you're one of the winners being part of their initial indoctrination and training; ie PS/Fate 1 as their free starting Sphere), they also work a lot with Mind (almost rivaling the NWO with it), Prime, Time, and Connection. Life also plays a part, usually completely focused on the self (biofeedback, diet and exercise regimes, and other methods of life extension). Matter usually doesn't get much above 1 (being able to judge the make and value of objects), and Forces and Spirit usually end up being Equipment/Talismans purchased from the other Conventions (except for certain people in Special Projects who work in those areas).<br /> <br /> By the by, my default set of Mage backgrounds (which I limit to 10) are Allies, Contacts, Followers, Influence, Library, Mentor, Resources, Sanctum, Status, and Talisman. These are changed for Technocrat characters as Associates, Favors, Staff, Leverage, Training, Patron, Backing, Lab, Rank, and Equipment, all to reflect how I have Union culture, politics, and organization work.<br /> <br /> As for the Conventions and their various Spheres/Sciences, they generally break down as:<ul><li>NWO: Mind (primary), followed mainly by Connection and Time. Life, Prime and Spirit.</li> <li>Iteration X: Matter (primary), followed closely by Forces and Life. Mind, Time and Connection.</li> <li>Progenitors: Life (primary), followed mostly by Mind and Matter. Prime and Spirit (almost entirely xenobiology)</li> <li>Syndicate: Fate (primary), follow closely by Mind and Prime. Time, Connection and Life.</li> <li>Void Engineers: Spirit (primary), followed closely by Connection and Time. Mind, Forces and Life.</li> </ul>With defectors, NWO usually ends up in Virtual Adepts (but sometimes Order of Hermes, Celestial Chorus or Akashic Brotherhood), ItX in Virtual Adepts (and sometimes Sons of Ether), Progenitors in Virtual Adepts or Ether (and sometimes Verbena or rarely Ecstatics), Syndicate in Virtual Adepts (and sometimes the Hermetic House Jerbiton), and Void Engineers in Sons of Ether (and sometimes Dreamspeakers).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 03-25-2020, 09:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-26T19:48:22-07:00","id":1376771,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1376469#post1376469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>The Syndicate</b><br /> <br /> [...]<br /> <br /> Syndicate strongholds include Offices, Tradehouses, Showrooms, Vaults, and Retreats.<br /> The ultimate end of Syndicate indoctrination, privilege and adjustments is to become one of The Elite, far above the common masses and their petty concerns or emotions.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> After reading you insights it just gave me an interesting idea. We already had treads talking about how hard would it be for players picking Syndicate as a Convention, because they are way too much social centered and would be hard to use in any other situation that involves money and bla bla bla. You actually grasped their true essece, that is not really about captalism, but self-development, adaptation elitism. Having an entire Convention just about money and economics makes little sense, but what about if they had something special amongst the others: <b>my idea is that every Syndicate member is a &quot;sponsor&quot; of sorts for at least one or more individuals from the other groups</b>. That would make more sense as to why they are still needed even when techonology alone would be enough to handle the finacial system. It goes perfectly with your idea, the Syndicate ALWAYS works to be in the better position, so why wouldnt them nurture the &quot;fate&quot; of their choosen golden goose? That woulod be interesting as a game mechanic because would make the Syndicate members more active, pursuing things along with their &quot;<b>investiments</b>&quot; to see if they are or maintain their &quot;<b>value</b>&quot;, if not, discarding them and getting something better in their place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24274,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24274-menos125","user":"menos125","date":". 03-26-2020, 10:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24274-menos125","name":"menos125","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-26T20:25:06-07:00","id":1376776,"title":"","content":"One of the cool things about the Syndicate is that their field agents have access to literally The Best Training and Tools Money Can Buy. That includes martial arts and gun training that rivals the Akashic Brotherhood and Euthanatos, elite espionage and spycraft, hacking and detective skills, the social skills to blend in anywhere, bullet proof clothing, cool gadgets, etc. All the stuff you see in John Wick, Equelibrium, Leverage, and the like, to effectively be a Black Widow with Tony Stark's money or a Batman motivated by his own sense of ego.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T17:04:33-07:00","id":1377604,"title":"","content":"<b>The Hollow Ones</b><br /> <br /> <i>This is a Dark Ride.</i><br /> - sign hung in John Hemingway's office, The John Larroquette Show<br /> <br /> So, to be perfectly honest, the Hollow Ones kind of bugged me at first. This was the summer or early fall of 1993 when Mage 1st ed originally came out, and they initially came across as something included just to remind everyone that this game was supposed to be set in the same world as Vampire, and so naturally had a group that seemed to embody every single annoying aspect of a certain segment of Anne Rice fandom and Francis Ford Coppola's Dracula including a &quot;Mina is Vlad's reincarnated true love&quot; romance angle. Their expansion in World of Darkness: Outcasts and Orphans' Survival Guide helped a little, but still skewed a bit toward style over substance, and didn't differentiate enough between the Hollowers and Orphans in general IMO. But at some point, they gradually grew on me. Part of it was that I knew a number of Goths over that decade, and certain presentations of the somewhere-between-Craft-and-Tradition explained them in a way that made sense and helped me understand how their philosophy and world view fit into not-necessarily-Goth subcultures (including the metal, horror, and indie comic scenes I was familiar with personally). And because I was able to find some many nooks and crannies of the world that they seemed to fit in, I was always kind of disappointed that they didn't seem to get the full expansion of internal factions and subfactions that other groups did. So I ended up making my own.<br /> <br /> <b>The Unseen Society</b><br /> Sometimes seen as the upper class or staid elders of the Hollow Ones, there are references to them as far back as the early 19th century. They tend to be intellectuals and scholarly types who value their privacy as they delve into the realms of arcane lore, forbidden knowledge, and hidden history. Their mystical stylings tend toward high ritual, spiritualism, and science.<ul><li><i>Grimm's Children</i>: A loose collective of folklorists, historians, and storytellers constantly on the hunt for stories both old and new which serve to illuminate the true nature of the World of Darkness. They pour over uncensored fairy tails, urban legends, and ghost stories, seeking the power and lore hidden within our modern mythology, finding potential spells, forgotten rituals, and strange spirits. Many of them specialize in local history, urban exploration, true crime and even gossip as they compete to see who can find the purist or most morbid version of a tale. Given their interests and activities, it's not uncommon for members to end up entangled in the machinations of the Fair Folk.</li> <li><i>The School of the Dark Arts</i>: Dating back at least to Regency England or pre-Revolutionary France, they are an elitist society of aesthetics with an appreciation for works of art considered dark, gothic, and morbid. Members enjoy collecting, discussing, and even creating works that shock and offend conventional sensibilities; However, banal and predictable attempts at blasphemy - a crucifix in urine or a statue of the Virgin Mary covered in animal dung, for example - are met only with distain and mockery. In addition to their artistic activities, members also engage in Luciferian Satanism, chaos magic, demonology, and tantra.</li> <li><i>The Eternal Mystery</i>: A collection of mostly amateur detectives and investigators devoted to solving the ultimate mysteries of death and the afterlife. Members often insert themselves into mundane murder investigations, especially cold cases, while also looking into hauntings and other Fortean events and actively debunking spiritualist frauds. Their tools include forensic science, parapsychology, and various forms of necromancy. Many are skilled exorcists and spirit mediums.</li> <li><i>The Spider's Web</i>: Technomancers who augment their computer and engineering skills with eclectic magical styles. They specialize in exploring the mundane Deep Web, the mystical Digital Web, and the underworld's Dark Web. Their hardware frequently incorporates crystals, relics, and bound spirits, while their software is often coded with arcane symbols and languages. Members know each other largely online, and gain prestige by uncovering hidden secrets, unlocking closed doors, and advancing the art of cyber-technomagic.</li> <li><i>The Councilors</i>: An oddity among the Hollow Ones, they are the political faction that wish to make the group a more organized force within the World of Darkness. Some go so far as to aspire to become the tenth Tradition and take a much more prominent role in the Ascension War. Those that take this path often work to learn all they can of Awakened history and society, going out of their way to join Tradition cabals or chantries. They often specialize in both defensive magics and Ars Cupidea.</li> </ul><br /> (Concepts: Walking encyclopedia of horror stories, Professional spirit medium, Collector of cursed objects, Cynical Satanic shock rocker, High-tech necromancer, Would be diplomat to the Traditions, True Crime writer specializing in cults, Alice in a Dark Wonderland.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 03-31-2020, 09:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T18:52:23-07:00","id":1377622,"title":"","content":"<b>The Danse Macabre </b><br /> What most people think of when they think of the Hollow Ones. The most social of the group, revelers in the dark underground scenes of the counterculture at least as far back as the early 20th century and the Lost Generation. Evolving among Bohemians, Beatniks, Punks, and Goths, the majority desire to live free and dark until they leave behind a pretty corpse. Their mystical style tends heavily toward Ars Cupidea augmented with spiritualism and techno-magic tricks.<ul><li><i>The Succubus Club</i>: Perhaps the most conforming to the Hollow One stereotype, their name derives from the infamous Chicago night club. They tend to mimic the fashions of various iterations of vampire styles, from the classically Gothic to the modern leather divas. Striving to be as sensual and seductive as possible, augmenting their Ars Cupidea with blood magic and demonology.</li> <li><i>The Nightingales</i>: Sirens of the night letting their dark souls shine through their music and performance. [Yes, marvel at my purple prose.] Members focus their magic through voice, dance, and musical instruments augmented with rituals and superstition. Their melodies and lyrics work their way into listeners minds like mnemonic viruses, manipulating emotions, dreams, and life itself. They seem evenly divided between those who join to form groups vs those who front acolytes so as not to share the spotlight.</li> <li><i>The Midnight Riders</i>: Diehard adherents of rockabilly and gothabilly, as well as its associated hot rod and motorcycle cultures. They roam the highways and byways in and between cities, seeking thrills and chills, often chasing urban legends and hoping to become one themselves. Their vehicles are typically augmented with various charms and even spiritual possession or awakening, while their bodies are covered with tattoos incorporating hidden mystical symbols.</li> <li><i>The Empty Heart</i>: Serial heartbreakers who master Ars Cupidea for dark ends, they are cruel and cynical anti-Cupids who hold out the promise of love only to rip it away at a moment calculated to cause maximum pain and sorrow. Motivated by a cynicism often born of either personal heartbreak or innate cruelty, they claim that they only show others the true harsh nature of reality. It's not uncommon for members to also master forms of alchemy, divination, and charm making, both classical and techno mystical.</li> <li><i>Klubkroft</i>: Originating in Germany's underground club scenes, technomages specializing in mass rituals disguised as raves, performance art, or flash mobs. Using psychotropic drugs, subliminal light shows, and subsonic frequencies mixed with spirit summonings, vision quests, and ritual sex, they try to shift the perspectives of attendees and performers alike. While this typically done to raise psychic awareness or support for social causes, events sometimes devolve into sessions of mass hypnosis or violent riots.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Singer with a voice that will break your heart and enslave your soul, Gothloli anti-Cupid, Dark-eyed fortune teller effecting an air of exotic mystery, Rave organizer with a hidden agenda, Last of the true old school punks, Leather-clad bondage queen, Drag Queen of the Damned, Byronic vampire hunter.)<br /> <br /> <b>The Darklings</b><br /> The most radical of the Hollow Ones, those who pursue lifestyles and paths of power far outside the mainstream of magic and mysticism. Prone to nihilism and self destruction, their quest for dark enlightenment often involves the realms of necromancy, blood magic and violence. They tend not to play well with others.<ul><li><i>The Dead Kids</i>: Necromancers who strive to keep at least one foot in the grave, walking among and even mimicking the dead. They dress in funeral garb and surround themselves with symbols of death, mourning, and decay while conducing seances, offering themselves up for possession, and raising corpses as walking dead. Many also delve into the dark arts of necrosynthesis. [See the revised Euthanatos Tradition book.]</li> <li><i>The Horror Show</i>: Fans of horror and splatter punk, self-styled freaks and monsters striving to master the powers of fear, revulsion and violence. Members use body modification and shapeshifting to reform their bodies into nightmarish insanities while also crafting poetically sadistic traps and mind-bending labyrinths for their prey. While many claim to be acting out a form of primal justice, punishing the guilty and abusing the abusers, others are more interested in simply avenging perceived personal slights or even terror for terror's sake.</li> <li><i>The Razors</i>: A cliquish cult of blood mages who channel their own personal pain and shed blood into mystical power. By mastering the mystical potential of their own blood, members are able to work powerful effects of blood sympathy to cast curses or enchantments, and even turn their own blood into deadly weapons or homunculi. Some go as far as to perform blood sacrifices for powerful magic rituals or summonings.</li> <li><i>The Bahari</i>: Also known as the Children of Lilith, this secretive cult of the Dark Mother is one of the more organized factions of Hollow Ones. Teaching a doctrine of enlightenment through pain, members work to shock the sensibilities and cause anguish among those they wish to &quot;teach&quot;, including themselves. They also work to balance creation with destruction, taking up such crafts as gardening or art, often with a dark bent including poisonous plants or grotesque imagery. Their group rituals tend involve inflicting each other with physical and emotional pain, followed by sharing what insights they've gained.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Disciple of the Dark Mother, Masked slasher out to punished everyone who made his life a living hell, Young man in love with a ghost, Mentally unstable lord of nightmares, Perfect poison flower, Body-mod fanatic experimenting with grotesque homemade cybernetics, Virtuoso director of bleeding edge guerrilla horror films acclaimed for their realism, Abuse survivor turning her pain into power.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-31T19:18:22-07:00","id":1377626,"title":"","content":"Of course, M20 differentiated the Hollow Ones from Orphans in a very different way: it moves the Orphans away from being ad-hoc magicians with at best a makeshift paradigm and turned them into mages who differed from those of the Traditions and Crafts <i>only</i> in the sense that they don't have memberships in any secret societies. This left the <i>Hollow Ones</i> as the advocates for what previous editions had presented as \u201cOrphan Magic\u201d \u2014 the ones who take the premise of \u201cWe're All Gods in Disguise\u201d to its logical conclusion and argue that the philosophical debates that bog everyone else down are ultimately pointless, and the differences that the various groups tout about each other are essentially window dressing. <br /> <br /> At least, that's how I run them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 03-31-2020, 10:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-07T15:58:37-07:00","id":1379346,"title":"","content":"My head canon (and this is largely based in M20):<br /> <br /> Fed up at constantly having their own concerns and needs ignored by the larger council, the Dreamspeakers withdraw from the Council of Nine (in our own game the events of Standing Rock served as the straw that broke the camel's back). And since they were largely made up of various diverse cultural groups that were really own combined in one tradition because many of the more...eurocentric traditions kinda shoved them together, refusing to recognize the diversity of the groups beyond &quot;deals with spirits&quot;, they officially disbanded.<br /> <br /> That's the official story at least. Unofficially a significant portion of the tradition actually remained together, secretly entering into membership talks with the Disparate Alliance. How they even knew of the existence of the Alliance isn't exactly clear.<br /> <br /> The Seat of Spirit wouldn't exactly remain empty for long however, for soon after the Dreamspeakers resign the Council, the Void Engineers defect, bringing grave news. The majority of the Technocracy is completely under the control of Nephandi infiltrators.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13838-hangedman1984","name":"hangedman1984","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13838_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-07T16:24:15-07:00","id":1379349,"title":"","content":"double post","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-07-2020, 08:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-07T17:40:03-07:00","id":1379361,"title":"","content":"Mmm\u2026 while I can see the Dreamspeakers leaving the Traditions (though I personally prefer the notion that they reorganize as the Khadavi; I <i>like</i> the Traditions having a faction of shamans in its ranks), I have a harder time seeing the Void Engineers abandoning the Technocracy, especially for the Traditions: their view of the basic metaphysics of the setting is very Technocratic, and very much at odds with the Traditions' \u201cWe Are All Gods in Disguise\u201d mindset; it was the Void Engineers' Tychoidean Cosmology that drove the Electrodyne Engineers out of the Union; and if they <i>were</i> to be accepted into the Traditions, it would be over the protest of the Society of Ether. Finally, the scenario where the Technocracy has been taken over by the Nephandi generally <i>doesn't</i> exempt the Void Engineers from this; it's just that the takeover of the Void Engineers tends to take the form of VEs who start sympathizing with the Umbrood more than they do with humans. <br /> <br /> On the other hand, I could see the upcoming Technocratic purge resulting in a disproportionate number of Void Engineers ending up with the breakaway \u201cCraft\u201d that may end up joining the Disparate Alliance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T07:17:27-07:00","id":1398775,"title":"","content":"The Sun is the Shard Realm of Prime - and is/was a key hotspot in the Ascension War, with many conflicts over its immense, potentially infinite supplies of Quintessence. The Technocracy's Void Engineers built many &quot;solar power collection stations&quot; in its orbits, and some Choristers view it as a manifestation of the One. It is unknown how Earth relates to the Shard Realms, with some mystics interpreting it as the Shard Realm of the Tenth Sphere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1410-czernobog","name":"Czernobog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-30T19:22:10-07:00","id":1400233,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://radio-noir.fandom.com/wiki/Victoria_Station\" target=\"_blank\">Here's what I've got so far for Victoria Station.</a><br /> <br /> Note that my game is currently in 1938 CE.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Any technology distinguishable from magic is insufficiently advanced.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12101-nitromidas","name":"Nitromidas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12101_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-04T18:28:50-07:00","id":1401031,"title":"","content":"I'm a fan of 'The Technocracy does have a huge hold on corporations + government' stuff. Never knew why they would settle for having Syndicate operatives in lower companies as opposed to: Tesla, Google, etc.<br /> Never understood why the NWO wouldn't have huge control over the government.<br /> Even the 'Good' version of revised Technocracy is very much controlling and they have the tools needed to get in there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T05:20:03-07:00","id":1401089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jihelu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1401031#post1401031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a fan of 'The Technocracy does have a huge hold on corporations + government' stuff. Never knew why they would settle for having Syndicate operatives in lower companies as opposed to: Tesla, Google, etc.<br /> Never understood why the NWO wouldn't have huge control over the government.<br /> Even the 'Good' version of revised Technocracy is very much controlling and they have the tools needed to get in there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why wouldn't they? Or rather, where does the idea they don't come from?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T06:59:39-07:00","id":1401106,"title":"","content":"If I recall correctly, <b>Guide to the Technocracy</b> made the point that the Technocracy's control over governments and corporations is far less extensive than they would have you believe \u2014 which is to say, the world's governments aren't all run by the NWO, and there are corporations that the Syndicate doesn't control. <br /> <br /> In many ways, it's the same issue as \u201cwhat kind of supernatural entity is [insert famous person here]\u201d? <br /> <br /> But in a homebrew or head-canon? Sure; why not? In particular, unless fame brings some sort of magical resistance with it, why <i>wouldn't</i> the rich and famous fall under the sway of various supernatural forces? They're just such tempting targets: mind control the local homeless man, and you get a mind-controlled homeless man: mind-control Elon Musk or Donald Trump, and what you can do extends much, much further. <b>Ascension's Landscape</b> addresses this issue in depth, along with a number of others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T09:41:19-07:00","id":1401138,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> My impression is that just meant they controlled the key government offices and groups and key businesses, (as opposed to all offices and every science and tech business) and that they sometimes didn't bother with infiltrating lesser organizations when they held more powerful ones that could influence those lesser ones. Not that they settled for having operatives in the lesser companies as mentioned above. That was my thinking.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-06T12:38:04-07:00","id":1401383,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1401138#post1401138\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> My impression is that just meant they controlled the key government offices and groups and key businesses, (as opposed to all offices and every science and tech business) and that they sometimes didn't bother with infiltrating lesser organizations when they held more powerful ones that could influence those lesser ones. Not that they settled for having operatives in the lesser companies as mentioned above. That was my thinking.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is outright a quote in the Revised Syndicate book (Maybe some stuff in NWO, I think Technocracy book in general goes 'They don't control the entire USA') about how they deliberately avoid controlling big companies (Like Tesla and stuff)<br /> The assumption is they /don't/ run the government.<br /> <br /> Sometimes I agree but, I like the idea of them controlling everything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-06T19:23:45-07:00","id":1401418,"title":"","content":"I don't recall reading that. About deliberately avoiding big companies. That sounds pretty counterintuitive too. I'll look that up. As for not controlling the U.S.A. well, you can't really control the whole country and remain secret so I see no problem there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-07T00:14:13-07:00","id":1401441,"title":"","content":"I was thinking today of maybe dropping the rule about abandoning instruments entirely. You spend a lot of time during character creation crafting this magickal system for your character, and learning the ins and outs of it as you play... occasionally you get to do some really interesting things with your magickal instruments. And the reward for using them is... abandoning them?<br /> <br /> I get the idea, that the power was within you all along, and you don't need all this stuff. But the rule makes instruments seem like a punishment, when I think they are part of the fun. What do you put in your magick potions? How do you craft your staff of lightning? What technology lets you read the minds of others? I would rather players put more thought into their instruments, and creative ways of using them, rather than counting down the days until they can finally be rid of them.<br /> <br /> Maybe instead, come up with a rule that every time they come up with a new effect, they can journal it and take note of what instrument they used. After a certain number of journaled instruments they can then &quot;master&quot; that instrument, and get a small bonus to arete rolls with that instrument.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-07T04:02:48-07:00","id":1401456,"title":"","content":"Remember that, when you become able to abandon an instrument, still using it gives you a -1 difficulty modifier to the casting.<br /> So technically, you still get awarded for using the instrument. You could increase the reward by changing that from -1 difficulty to +1 dice, since -1 diff is easy to obtain through other means, while normally the Arete dice are a hard limit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-08T10:34:19-07:00","id":1401709,"title":"","content":"I have been using an HR allowing mages to get bonus dice for using multiple instruments in a rote or ritual. The result has been that players disregard &quot;discarding&quot;. Instead, they prefer to roleplay how they incorporate their Instruments to get a bonus die or two.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-12T15:02:22-07:00","id":1402477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1360777#post1360777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Too many to mention, as Mage was one of the first games I started taking apart and rebuilding after Time of Judgment. Unfortunately, I lost the entire post I spent a good half hour writing, and don't feel like trying to rewrite it right now. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I will mention that when doing Mage: The Dark Ages (as opposed to DA:M), I use the usual modern day magic system and my own Fellowships. (The Subtle Ones, The Gardeners of the Tree of Life, the Craftmasons, the Memento Mori, The Keepers of the Sacred Fire, The Nine Sacred Passions, and The Voices of Mercy), with the Pax Hermetica and the Society of Pure Thought being their own stand alone things.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd love to hear more about this. I love the Fellowship thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-12T21:21:34-07:00","id":1402522,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Archivist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1402477#post1402477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd love to hear more about this. I love the Fellowship thing.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1369694#post1369694\">Part One: General Set Up &amp; The Craftmasons, Gardeners, and Keepers of the Sacred Fire</a> <br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1374837#post1374837\">Part Two: The Memento Mori and the Sacred Passion</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1375217#post1375217\">Part Three: The Subtle Ones and The Voices of Mercy</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1375511#post1375511\">Part Four: The Sword of Gabriel, The Pax Hermetica &amp; Minor Factions</a><br /> <br /> <br /> Outside of Europe and the Middle East would be<ul><li>the Great Stone Court/House of Stone in Southern Africa, centered in Madzimbabwe and including factions that in the modern day would be parts of the Euthanatos, the Dreamspeakers, the Ngoma, the Ahl I Batin, and probably the Cult of Ecstasy, Verbena, and Celestial Chorus as well. (West Africa has a number of kingdoms, most of which have trade networks to North Africa and Islamic cultural influences, giving them ties to the Web of Faith in addition to their own independent groups. The mages of the isolated Christian Zagwe kingdom in Ethiopia are probably their own independent thing.)</li> <li>the Ministry of Works in Song China, overseen by what would become the Wu Lung and having authority over groups/mages who are part of what are now the Akashic Brotherhood, the Cult of Ecstasy, the Dreamspeakers, and various Crafts, as well as the equivalent to Artificers, alchemists, Syndicate-style merchants, and others.</li> <li>the Vedic/Hindu alliance in the Indian subcontinent (Sorcerers' Crusade and other material refers to them as the Asthika, but that's a Sanskrit term for one who believes in the concept of the inner soul and of the greater universal principles, so while that's a valid word for what the members are, it's not all that great as the name of an organization or alliance) that includes various Thanatoic and Ecstatic groups, as well as factions that would become parts of the Dreamspeakers, Celestial Chorus, and Akashic Brotherhood, and various Crafts.</li> <li>Japan is at the period when it's transitioning from the classical Heian dynasty to the Kamakura shogunate, probably represented by two rival &quot;houses&quot; of Awakened, each including various members who would be Akashics, Dreamspeakers, various porto-technocrats, and other groups.</li> <li>If you wanted to do anything with the Americas in this period, this is when the Iroquois League is founded, the time of the Mississippian, Fort Ancient and Plains Village cultures, and the start of the Great Pueblo period. In Mesoamerica, the Toltec period is coming to an end, and the Mexica/Aztecs haven't really arrived yet. The Andes are in a transitional period, with a few previously existing empires/cultures having declined or collapsed about 100 years ago, while the Inca are still another 100 or so years off in the future.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-13T06:58:00-07:00","id":1402591,"title":"","content":"This bad boy needs to be an SV supplement","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-13T07:14:20-07:00","id":1402592,"title":"","content":"What are these groups pillars and foundations? Did you make new ones?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-13T08:20:32-07:00","id":1402606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Archivist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1402592#post1402592\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What are these groups pillars and foundations? Did you make new ones?</div> </div> </div> </div>I just use the modern Sphere systems, both for simplicity and because it allows me to have new factions without having to invent totally new mechanics for every different group or non-European region.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-13T08:31:16-07:00","id":1402607,"title":"","content":"How do you get them to feel foundation + pillar-y. The positive of that system is that the different paradigms really feel very different, not just a thin veneer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-13T11:13:18-07:00","id":1402639,"title":"","content":"If you insist on using Foundations and Pillars, then most of them map to the existing ones. The Subtle Ones are the Ahl I Batin just with all the Islamic elements stripped out so that they can carry out the Doctrine of Unity without issue in medieval Europe. Gardeners = Old Faith; Keepers = Spirit Talkers; Voices of Mercy (and Sword) = Messianics. Memento Mori can probably adapt the ones from the Black Hand, sort of. You're pretty much on your own for the Sacred Passion. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I've just always treated the Spheres as an objective fundamental truth of how Reality and the Tapestry work, (ultimately reflecting the ideal that people are more alike than they are different) with how each faction treats and interprets those truths/rules as being the cornerstones for their overall philosophies and paradigms. Just personally, I feel that these things should be something that requires a degree of thought and reading, as opposed to just a bunch of totally separate rules. To me, the &quot;why&quot; has always been more important than the &quot;what&quot;. (I also tend to more strongly emphasize the use of Rotes as specific &quot;spells&quot;/rituals that are passed down through different traditions and reflecting their cultures and beliefs.)<br /> On top of this, I just really don't have the time or inclination to try to work out five totally new ranked powers for every faction I decide to include, especially if I decide to have a game involving the Web of Faith or some place outside of Medieval Europe. While I'm fairly well read in my old age, I don't consider myself enough of an expert on Sufi asceticism or medieval Hinduism or Bantu culture or so forth to develop something that actually reflects what those people would believe. Compounding this is my long running desire to keep things as simple as possible in terms of mechanics so as to keep the game's story and character roleplaying flowing as smoothly as I can without getting bogged down in mechanical issues. <br /> <br /> To be perfectly honest, I've long considered Dark Ages: Mage to be a game that had no reason to exist other than to show off a shiny new mechanical system the writers and developer had come up with. As written, nothing is really going on that drives the setting the way the War of Princes does Dark Ages: Vampire, the start of the Ascension War drives Mage: The Sorcerers' Crusade, and the Storm-eater and &quot;Second&quot; War of Rage drive Werewolf: The Wild West (or how current events drove the modern games). And it's a new system that's inherently limiting. You can't play medieval members of the Cult of Ecstasy, for example. And even some of the Hermetic Houses are impossible to do with the existing Forma. You can - finally - kludge your way into playing Mediterranean proto-Thanatoics using the rules from the V20 Black Hand sourcebook, but if you want to play a medieval Akashic, your only hope is to try to dig up a rough draft writer Malcolm Shepherd put up on the internet nearly 20 years ago. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-14T10:09:44-07:00","id":1402822,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1361547#post1361547\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Still think that makes no sense. Why is the faction that's even less organized or willing to work with each other than The Traditions winning?<br /> <br /> They regularly kill each other off because of infighting, are split into their own warring sub-factions that hate each other on principle, are mercilessly hunted by the 2 most powerful organizations in their setting, have a meat grinder of a training program that means apprentices are unlikely to live long enough to see arete 3, and recruit their numbers exclusively from individuals with the rarest trait in setting(an awakening).<br /> <br /> The nephandi should honestly be as one step above being disparate as orphans or individual crafts. How in the qliphoth did they scrape together the resources, leadership, planning, and discipline needed to infiltrate the largest and most organized collection of the most powerful magic users with the most advanced science and magic the world has too offer... you know, unless they just use plot armor and authorial bias.<br /> (;..)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's quite an astute observation. Going by the <i>Book of Madness</i> 2nd edition, the Nephandi seem to be of somewhat better quality than other Mages to me. If you read between the lines, the Nephandi ranks are superior to other Mages in various ways. The Adsinistrati are tempters beyond compare and can &quot;r<i>each gilledean heights of power</i>&quot;. Another quote: &quot;<i>Few Mages ever meet a Nephandus of rank higher than adsinistratus, but generally that's more than enough.</i>&quot; The Prelati negotiate deals with the Traditions and the Technocracy. And there is also this part &quot;<i>There have been perhaps a half-dozen cases in the past 30 years when prelati actually got their hands dirty in fieldwork, but each time the repercussions of the events echoed for decades afterwards.&quot;</i> or &quot;<i>Most prelati travel alone, but all can summon help almost instantly.&quot;</i> Shaytans are compared to SWAT teams.<br /> There is also the part in which the Nephandi are able to reliably and swiftly find Widderslaintes to recruit them into the Nephandi.<br /> <br /> The fact that the Nephandi faction exists, despite being in direct and stark opposition to the Traditions and the Technocracy, does hold an implication that they have, under all of their infighting, enough of organization, consistency, and individual capability, to regularly come out at the top.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-19T14:25:02-07:00","id":1403570,"title":"","content":"Because it came up in another thread, I tend to organize the Cult of Ecstasy a little differently than what you see in the revised tradition book (or the list of factions in their revised write up). Rather than organized factions, such as what you see in the the Akashic Brotherhood for example, the cult has four broad groupings generally based on the kamamarga/paths of ecstasy favored by the sects that fall under each banner. While those sects may have leaders, and may or may not get along with others who fall under that banner, these broad factions have no centralized leadership. In fact, trying to lead the Cult is akin to trying to herd cats, and is usually only accomplished via incredible reputation and force of personality. <br /> <br /> <b>The Seekers of the Divine</b><br /> Striving for unity and sacred congress with higher cosmic powers, spirits and forces, they touch the divine World Pulse and channel the powers of gods into reality.<ul><li><i>The Dance of Shiva</i> - One of the oldest sects, originating in India and now strongly tied with Shivaism. Using left and right hand Tantra, meditation, and powerful yoga techniques, they channel and harness powerful male and female energies in pursuit of destruction and rebirth.</li> <li><i>The Pythian Mysteries</i> - Descended from the Oracles of Delphi and their attendant cult of Apollo. Famed prophets and seers who channel the spirits of their god through ingestion of herbal, alchemical, and - in more modern times - chemically created mixtures.</li> <li><i>The Darwushim</i> - Mystical dervishes of Central and Southwest Asia and North Africa, with ties to both the Ahl I Batin and Akashic Brotherhood. Sufi ascetics who seek unity between the material world and the divine by stripping away the illusions of life and touching the power of Allah.</li> <li><i>The K'un Lu</i> - Chinese practitioners of Taoist alchemy and Tantric yoga. They alternate cultivating and purifying their own Yin and Yang energies with the ultimate goal of uniting the two to achieve enlightenment, immortality and Ascension.</li> <li><i>The Silver Bridge</i> - Christian ecstatics devoted to pursing the Nine Sacred Passions as a path to divine revelation and communion with the Holy Spirit. Methods differ according to denominational influence, but includes the use of sacred icons, music, dance, fasting, and taking up serpents.</li> <li><i>The Serpent Path</i> - Originating in West Africa and from the syncretic faiths of Brazil and the Caribbean, especially Haitian Voudoun. Members use ritual song, dance, sacrifice, and pain to summon and bond with the Loa in sacred congress and take their power as their own.</li> <li><i>The Moonchasers</i> - Modern Wiccan-influenced ecstatics. They use chanting, ritual sex, hallucinogens, and sacred masks to channel the Triple Goddess, the Horned Man, and nature spirits in order to cultivate their own inner divinity.</li> </ul><b>The Bringers of Joy</b><br /> Chasing after pleasure and joy as a path to Lankashim, they work to spread ecstasy and uplift individuals, communities, and the entire world through the simple pleasures of life.<ul><li><i>The Asparsas</i> - The &quot;Daughters of Pleasure&quot;, an all female sect of Hindu Shaktists. Dakini mistresses of Tantra, yoga, and the Karma Sutra, they are skilled ceremonial ritualists and users of Ars Cupidae. They also act as quieters of the restless dead and as divine shield maidens of the demon-slayer goddess Durga.</li> <li><i>The Fellowship of Pan</i> - Inheritors of the ancient traditions of Dionysus, revitalized by the Romantic and Bohemian movements. Devoted to lives of wine, women (and men and ...) and song, intellectual and philosophical discussion, music, art, dance, drama and poetry. They channel the powers of ancient gods, the Muses and Graces, and nature spirits in pursuit of the ideal of Arete.</li> <li><i>The Feather Flowers</i> - Originating with Mesoamerican Mayan fertility cults, the handmaidens of Xochiquetzal blend ancient shamanism with Catholic mysticism and bruja arts. Members act as healers, midwives, matchmakers, and protectors of children while also comforting the restless dead and trying to calm societal violence.</li> <li><i>L'Desir Archane</i> - Born out of the 18th century French Libertines and their associated British Hellfire Clubs, they hold that Pleasure and Pain are two sides of the same coin in the quest for Lankashim. Specializing in bondage, S&amp;M culture, sex, Tantric ritual, and philosophies of dominance and submission, they're also known for summoning dangerous spirits - including demons - for the purpose of sacred congress.</li> <li><i>Schaffensfreude</i> - Perhaps one of the least organized sects, members focus themselves on the simple joys of creation and craftsmanship. Individuals pursue their own preferred pursuits, ranging from gardening and cooking to motorcycle mechanics and fine art, trying to strike a balance between the rational worldview necessary to master their craft with the romantic joy of creation. Through this balance and the pleasure they derive from it, they are able to touch Lankashim and often infuse their works with magical energy and effects.</li> <li><i>The Wild Boyz (and Girlz)</i>- Developing from California's post WW2 subcultures of motorcycles, hot rodding and surfing, today they are a collective of extreme athletes, daredevils, thrill seekers and ravers. They use danger and adrenaline to &quot;get high on life&quot; and &quot;into the zone&quot; of the World Pulse.</li> <li><i>Klubwerks</i> - Ultramodernist techno-ecstatics who evolved from 19th century Terpsichorean music hall performers, Weimar era cabarets, and 70s discotheques. Heavily involved in the underground club scenes of various cities, they use high tech dance music, light shows, and designer drugs to create mass rituals of consciousness expansion.</li> </ul><b>The Sowers of Discord</b><br /> Rebels and revolutionaries using chaos to break down the barriers erected by modern regimental society in their quest for esoteric enlightenment and psycho-spiritual freedom.<ul><li><i>The Maenads</i> - An ancient and slowly dying cult of angry and often violent women. They engage in monthly full-moon rituals in which members work themselves into a violent ecstatic frenzy through sacred wine (and sometimes harder drugs), loud music and dancing, culminating in an animal sacrifice being town apart with bare hands and its raw flesh eaten. Sometimes this sacrifice is a human - usually male - who is judged to have committed unpardonable sins against the cult or womanhood in general. The rest of the month, members work as activists and crusaders against the Patriarchy, working magic via psychedelic vision quests and sexual ritual.</li> <li><i>The Hagalaz</i> - Nordic seidr witches and Loki worshipers devoted to spreading chaos and undermining the order of society. Militantly anti-Christian and often accused of ties to various neo-Nazi groups, they are currently heavily involved in Northern Europe's various biker cultures and heavy metal fandoms. They are skilled illusionists and masters of mind control, as well as shapeshifters and necromancers.</li> <li><i>The 5th World Tribe</i> - A mix of Southwestern peyote shaman and 1960s Hippy acid-tripping anarchists advocating a more &quot;natural&quot; and spiritually oriented lifestyle. Trying to subvert society through personal example, most of them live off the grid in pre-digital or pre-industrial communes or nomadic bands. They reach Lankashim via music, dance and vision quests, and are also fairly famous for their handcrafts (especially fetish talisman).</li> <li><i>The Dissonance Society</i> - Intellectual anarchist philosophers and radical libertarians, they originated in the salons of the French Enlightenment and came to fruition with the 20th century Beatnik and academic culture. Members fight the Ascension War in the realm of ideas, writing articles, books and manifestos on various sociopolitical topics while also engaging in various psychonaut experiments involving biofeedback, LSD, and sensory deprivation.</li> <li><i>The Lightbringers</i> - Modern Luciferian Satanists advocating absolute personal freedom, objective morality, intellectual clarity and iron-clad self-discipline while breaking societal taboos in order to escape the chains of group think. Members use the Black Mass and other calculatedly offensive materials in their ritual chaos magic and demonology.</li> <li><i>Bongo's Rangers</i> - Punk rock renegades founded and inspired by 1970s radical performance artist and musician Depak Bongo. Modern members pursue high-tech anarchism through wild outlaw raves, digital terrorism, and chemically engineered designer drugs that tie users brains in knots and break down the rational underpinnings of reality.</li> <li><i>The Chronoknights</i> - A small but fanatical group of chaos technomancers in the United Kingdom. Borderline Maraduers who believe that linear time itself is an unnecessary chain upon the mind and society that prevents enlightenment, Lankashim, and Ascension. Through use of chaos magic ritual and cutting edge theoretical physics, they summon strange and powerful time spirits in order to tear themselves out of the flow of time and subvert the laws of cause and effect.</li> </ul><b>The Destroyers of Flesh</b><br /> Extremists seeking enlightenment and power through obliteration of all moral and physical limits, pushing themselves to near destruction in an effort to extend their own breaking points.<ul><li><i>The Kiss of Astarte</i> - A female mystery cult originating with the ancient Bronze Age worship of the Mesopotamian fertility goddess Astarte (aka Ianna, Ishtar, Cybele, and Aphrodite) and her torturous journey through the Underworld. Members preach a doctrine of female empowerment through tribulation, revenge, and sexual power. Their rites include rituals of sacred harlotry and the castration of male attendants.</li> <li><i>The Eight Chains</i> - A sect of Hindu Shivaists derived from the Aghori ascetic traditions. Members believe that the soul is one with Shiva, but is bound by eight fetters which must be removed in order to be free of the cycle of reincarnation. In addition, as Shiva is all good and all things are of Shiva, nothing is inherently unclean or taboo. Members use left-hand Tantra, sexual rituals, associate with death and the dead, self-flagellation, the taking of animal forms, and other methods to destroy their own sense of shame, fear, lust, greed and other chains. This will often involve behavior that many would consider antisocial, including rudeness, vulgarity, casual cruelty, and violence. Ironically, their rejection of taboos also means that they are willing to openly associate with and aide society's stigmatized and neglected, including street children, those with diseases such as leprosy or AIDS, the mentally ill, and other unwanted and Untouchables. More than a few help operate medical clinics, orphanages, and other projects.</li> <li><i>The Studiosi</i> - A radical offshoot of the medieval Gnostic Cathars and flagellants, fanatically devoted to the destruction of the modern Rex Mundi that is the Technocracy and all of its fronts. Members work to overcome the prison of material existence through extreme fasting, scourging, near-death experiences, and other forms of self-torture. They are also extremely effective terrorists in the Ascension War.</li> <li><i>Los Sangradores</i> - Mesoamerican shaman who preserve ancient Mayan and Toltec bloodletting rituals, using their own blood and pain as a sacrifice to the gods, enabling one to commune with the spirit world and move outside of time. Over the centuries, members have begun to incorporate various aspects of Catholic rituals, mysticism and trappings into their rites so as to more easily move among mundane people.</li> <li><i>The LeMarchand Club</i> - An extremist offshoot of L'Desire Archane founded in 1970s Los Angeles. Members no longer differentiate between the sensations of pleasure and pain, using magic to torture themselves and each other to extremes that would otherwise be impossible to survive in an effort to experience the ultimate in ecstatic bliss. Most are completely addicted to the sensations they experience.</li> <li><i>The Heart of the Void</i> - One of the strangest and more obscure sects. Members strive to reach Lankashim through the complete absence of sensation. To this end, they experiment endlessly with Zen and transcendental meditation, biofeedback, sensory deprivation, and self isolation. It's rumored that some who master this path simply vanish from reality.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-19T14:52:44-07:00","id":1403576,"title":"","content":"This is really cool","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-20T06:45:52-07:00","id":1403657,"title":"","content":"In a similar vein, I prefer to recast the Euthanatoi as two Traditions: one (the Chakravanti) that's similar to the Tradition as published, but with the necromancy stuff largely excised: they still include the \u201cGood Death assassins\u201d notion, though that's less a matter of what they're all about than what they're known for (in truth, it's just one aspect of what they do; but it's such a <i>controversial</i> aspect that it's all anyone else ever talks about); but actual interactions with the Underworld and the dead isn't in their bailiwick any more than it is for nearly any other Tradition. Factions include the eponymous Chakravanti, the Aided (geasa <i>can</i> be about death, but are generally about so much more), a Greco-Roman sect that takes its cues from the Fates, and the Valdaermen. <br /> <br /> The other Tradition (the Euthanatoi) is <i>all about</i> necromancy; but other than that and the name, it has little in common with the published Euthanatos. <i>These</i> Euthanatoi are more of a \u201cWraith crossover Tradition\u201d that deals with the Dark Umbra and its denizens. Its views tend toward ancestor veneration; and where the living are concerned, it tends to focus more on the notion of the Wisdom of the Ages and learning from what has gone before. Its factions tend to correspond to the various Dark Kingdoms that are featured in <b>Wraith</b>: the Heirochthonoi roughly parallel the Dark Kingdom of Iron; the Madzimbabwe similarly parallel the Dark Kingdom of Ivory; the Bata'a correspond to Les Invisibles; other factions correspond to the Dark Kingdoms of Jade and Obsidian (China and Mesoamerica, respectively), the City of Delights (India), the Kingdom of Clay (Australia), and the Sea Which Knows No Sun (Polynesia). There's also an Egyptian faction that draws upon the same mythology as <b>Mummy</b>, and other factions corresponding to other cultures. Each faction has its own take on the nature of the Afterlife, as detailed in the various Dark Kingdom write-ups.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-20T13:37:46-07:00","id":1403708,"title":"","content":"Al-Aswad and his lieutenants (as detailed in <i>Hell on Earth</i>) were slain during the Sundering of Berlin in 1945. However, the Nephandi have faith (and the Traditions deeply fear) that they have or soon will reincarnate as <i>Widderslainte</i> of immense dark potential.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1410-czernobog","name":"Czernobog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-21T11:55:53-07:00","id":1403855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Czernobog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1403708#post1403708\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Al-Aswad and his lieutenants (as detailed in <i>Hell on Earth</i>) were slain during the Sundering of Berlin in 1945. However, the Nephandi have faith (and the Traditions deeply fear) that they have or soon will reincarnate as <i>Widderslainte</i> of immense dark potential.</div> </div> </div> </div>i dont play the game, but I can see one of the nephandi oracles reincarnated and wanting to be redeemed. the redemption seeking oracle would be a player character, while the main antagonists would be the other oracles, who have embraced there evil and want to bring about hell on earth. <br /> <br /> also, on the subject of nephandi redemption my headcanon is that the <i>Widderslainte </i>are the easiest to redeem. This is because unlike the barabbus they didn't choose to be evil, they suffer the pastsins and have a literal devil on there shoulder in the form of the twisted avatar. Of course, it is still a very hard thing. nephandic redemption is always the subject of a truly legendary quest. and of course the ease of redemption means jack squat if the nephandi doesn't want to be redeemed. so few nephandi do want redemption, as they range from cartoonish superviliany to the very personal and very real horrors of abusers and such. given those choices, so few nephandi want to be better, and those that do have all failed in truly epic fashions that nephandic redemption is considered a pipe dream chased by over idealistic mages who can somehow love the darkest evil with there sanity intact or mages one step away from mauraderhood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26173-evtrax","name":"evtrax","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"228"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-21T12:37:30-07:00","id":1403863,"title":"","content":"The Sphinx is one of the first mages in history with an essence of infinity that is trying to keep the unnamed and his masters from destroying the creation.<br /> <br /> Threat Null are the remnants of another world, where the Nephandi succesfully made the corrupted Technocracy sacrifice it to their cthulhuesque masters and now the survivers arrive in this world to let their gods feast on another world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>As I am from Austria I need to clarify two things.<br /> First my native language is german and so please point out if the english I write is broken so I can improve.<br /> Second I do not own VTMV beyond first three books nor any line after M20 Corebook because it is not out there and I wait for the translation.","edited":{"userid":26160,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26160-koronus","user":"Koronus","date":". 08-11-2020, 11:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26160-koronus","name":"Koronus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-18T15:03:51-07:00","id":1414476,"title":"","content":"So, the Celestial Chorus.<br /> <br /> So, my take on the Chorus has long been that they are not synonymous with any real world religion. Instead, their belief is that The One and his/her/its relation to real world religions is akin to that of the Akashic Brotherhood in regards to Do and its relationship with real world martial arts. Which is to say, the Chorus believes that it is the original prototype ur-religion and that mundane/real world faiths are &quot;Gateway Faiths&quot; to The One in the same way that mundane/real world martial arts are Gateway Arts to Do. <br /> Primarily, these Gateway Faiths have tended to be monotheistic ones, especially those from along the Indian Rim and eastern Mediterranean Sea. This includes certain forms of Hinduism, Zoroastrianism, Judaism, and Atenism, and later various faiths that have grown out of them, such as Sikhism, Baha'i, Mithraism, Manichaeism, Islam, Christianity, and Gnosticism. (In more than a few ways, the Chorus is very similar to a Gnostic mystery cult.) It also finds expression through the faiths of the Yoruba, the Algonquian, and others. In addition, there are a number of syncretic faiths that also serve as Gateway Faiths for the Chorus, including Seicho no li, Cao Dei, Rastafarianism, and certain interpretations of different African diaspora religions. But all of these are ultimately just seen as starting points to a &quot;higher truth&quot; about Reality and Creation. <br /> <br /> The Sun is a major symbol of The One, being as it is the source of light and life just as The One is. Fire is another symbol, both as light and life, but also for purification. And then there's the concept of sacred sound/language. This is the Song of The One. It is the universal ohm, the Word of God, the language which spoke Reality into being. When members of the Chorus use magic, they are tapping into that power. <br /> <br /> The ultimate goal of every member of the Chorus is to reunite with The One (ie, Ascension). Because all human beings are the creation of The One, all are equally worthy of love and respect. And those that are hurt or otherwise fallen away from the light must be helped back into it. (Although sometimes this means tough love or accepting that one must be purified through judgment in the next life.) They also strive to bring human society back in tune with The One and its Song. Hopefully, this will ultimately bring about the Sacred Shining City, the Chorus's ideal utopian society in which everyone is free to hear the Song of The One and strive for divine reunification. In stark contrast to the Society of Pure Thought (and its modern incarnation of the NWO) ands its idea of all humanity singing the exact same note, the Chorus believes in a, well, chorus of endless voices, singing the Song of The One each in their own way but all still in harmony. <br /> <br /> In spite of the difficult relationship the Chorus and the Verbena have had over the centuries, my own head canon is that Valoran and Nightshade became friends during the Mistridge Tribunals and especially from his kindness in the wake of William Groth's death. <br /> <br /> And then there's the various internal factions. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-18T15:08:12-07:00","id":1414478,"title":"","content":"Factions of the Celestial Chorus (pt 1)<br /> <br /> <b>The Sacred Ministry</b> - The political and theological leadership of the Chorus, they are priests, preachers and prophets working to guide the world back in to harmony with The One and establish the Sacred City. They favor a mix of high ritual and fiery charismatic presence.<ul><li><i>The Golden Throne</i> are the elites of the Chorus, many of them holding positions of power and influence within both the Tradition and in mundane society. They are bishops, Brahmans, and deacons advancing the creation of the Sacred City through business, politics, philanthropy, and media. Most members are skilled in mundane and mystical social arts (including Ars Cupidae) as well as sacred oaths and powerful rites.</li> <li><i>The Burning Bush</i> are radical social activists focused on the middle and lower classes. They call their followers to live lives of righteousness, social justice, and compassion. They are powerful orators and able to work charismatic miracles to foster hope and change. However, more than a few are also prone to flights of demagoguery and zealotry.</li> <li><i>The White Doves</i> are the Chorus's formal diplomats and heralds among the Traditions. They serve as missionaries and apologists while advancing the Chorus's interests and furthering the creation of the Sacred City. In addition to Ars Cupidae, they are also well known for their travel and defensive magics.</li> <li><i>The Song of the Goddess</i> is an alliance of feminist voices advocating that The One is a feminine being/concept and force. They promote what they see as matriarchal teachings and values in both mundane society and the plans for the Sacred City. In addition to Christian Mariology, they also include those who incorporate various African beliefs as well as those who follow modern Wicca.</li> <li><i>The Sacred Blood</i> is a quasi-secret society claiming to protect and husband a number of sacred or divine bloodlines. Most of these are found in Europe, India and South America. Their hope is the creation of modern messiahs who will help lead the Chorus to victory in the Ascension War and help establish the Sacred City. Most of them have some skill in Life magic.</li> <li><i>The Gabrielites</i> are a small and cliquish society originally founded by members of the Sacred Order of Pure Thought who were given sanctuary by the Chorus after Technocratic purges. They still advocate the idea of One World, One Faith and the idea that a global theocracy devoted to The One is the best way to lead humanity to the Sacred City. Obviously, they tend to be frustrated that very few people listen to their ideas.</li> </ul><br /> <b>The Aegis Kai Doru (Shield &amp; Spear)</b> - The guardian orders of the Chorus, Paladins, crusaders, and sacred guardians sworn to protect and defend not only their Tradition, but also the innocent and the helpless against the depredations of the Technocracy and of the forces of Darkness. All are trained warriors and skilled in martial magics.<ul><li><i>The Sons of Mithra</i> are a fraternal fellowship that traces their origins to both the Roman Mithraic mysteries and to the earlier Persian Zoroastrianism. Dauntless warriors of truth and light against the forces of deceit and wickedness, members live by strict codes of personal and group honor. They are renowned as skilled diplomats, healers and monster slayers, and for their signature gladii and maces.</li> <li><i>The Sisters of Gabrielle</i> are a society of warrior women founded in Hellenic Greece by their namesake. Members strive to master both martial and spiritual arts in order to safeguard the material world from threats beyond the Gauntlet, including hostile spirits, demons, and the Nephandi. They are as famous for their skills as exorcists and seers as they are for their combat ability.</li> <li><i>The Sword of Vishnu</i> is an ancient and sacred order of Hindu Vaishnavist Kshatriyas. Each of them has a sacred dharma to battle wickedness and evil while striving for unity with the universal ohm of The One. They train in both ancient and modern forms of warfare, while also studying mystic yoga and the healing arts.</li> <li><i>The Carriers of the Shield</i> are a Shi'ite sect of sober Sufi mystics and scholars. They work to protect and guide their communities as teachers, lawyers, healers, exorcists, and - if needed - warriors.</li> <li><i>The Knights of St. George</i> are a secretive order of Gnostic-Christian warrior monks originating from the Crusades. Keepers of the hidden Goetic Gospels, members learn how master their own sinful urges and to combat the sin of others. They have gained a reputation as slayers of vampires, werewolves, and other monsters.</li> <li><i>The Ksirafari</i> are an incredibly secretive order of spies, undercover agents, and assassins. Their origins are unknown to outsiders, but are believed to be from the ancient near east. Members invoke the names and powers of the darker angels of the Heavenly Host, as well as practicing Ars Cupidae, sacred alchemy and technomagic.</li> <li><i>The Septarian Militias</i> are a loose alliance of prepper and survivalist groups striving to separate themselves from the modern government leviathan. Members and their acolytes live off the grid in self sufficient compounds, pursuing lives of simple righteousness while amassing sizable amounts of modern and mystical firepower.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-18T15:56:25-07:00","id":1414481,"title":"","content":"Part 2<br /> <br /> <b>The Alexandrian Society</b> - Scholars and philosophers who have tirelessly worked since the Hellenic age to discover and preserve ancient knowledge and divine wisdom, as well as to seek out and explore new manifestations of The One in the modern world. While perhaps the most bookish members of the Chorus, their sacred calling requires them to journey out into the world and face whatever dangers stand between them and the lore they seek.<ul><li><i>The Librum Sanctum</i> is the oldest and purest faction of the Society, created among the Library of Alexandria. They recover, restore and safeguard ancient works of history, philosophy, theology, science, and magic, and well as collect sacred relics and artifacts. Not just librarians and archaeologists, but teachers and smugglers who believe that humanity's past and its ideas are a vital cornerstone of the Sacred City.</li> <li><i>The University of Light</i> originates with the ancient Roman Collegium Praecepti and has almost as ancient ties with the lost Ahl I Batin. They believe that science, technology and philosophy - both modern and esoteric - allow one to study Creation and through it gain a better understanding of The One's grand design and purpose. Members are famous as kabbalists, theurgists, alchemists and astronomers, as well as for their skills in modern science and technomagic.</li> <li><i>Dues Ex Machina</i> is the tongue in cheek name for a collective of ultramodernist coders, mathematicians and engineers seeking the Song of The One in the Digital Web and modern technology. In addition, they work to use the Internet, telecommunication, and counterculture media to advance the creation of the Sacred City and spread the Chorus's message of harmony.</li> <li><i>The Collegium Solomon</i> is an elite society of theurgists and demonologists following the secret teachings and lore of King Solomon the Wise. They collect the True Names of various spirits and other beings so as to command them in the Chorus's name and bring them into accordance with the will of The One.</li> </ul><br /> <b>The Hands of Mercy</b> - Healers and peacemakers advocating The One as a source of succor and of universal brotherhood. They believe that mercy and compassion must be the foundation of the Sacred City, lest it be a castle built on sand. They view their calling as being society and spiritual as well as physical, and so are often skilled in Mind, Spirit and Life magic.<ul><li><i>The Nashimites</i> originated as a dualistic heresy in the Middle East. They believe that if enough of Creation turns to darkness and evil, The One will fall into darkness with them. Because of this, they are devoted to doing all within their power to rescue and redeem even the most fallen soul (even the Nephandi if possible). In this way, they hope to bring healing to the world and make the Sacred City a place of redemption.</li> <li><i>The Black Madonnas</i> originated as an Egyptian-Roman Isis cult and adapted to devotion to the Virgin Mary. Skilled healers, confessors and exorcists, they primarily focusing on helping the Restless Dead find peace and return to The One.</li> <li><i>The Chalice of Succor</i> takes inspiration from the legends of the Holy Grail. They combine faith, prayer and spiritual healing with modern medicine and psychology to help all in need, especially with the hidden hurts that Western medicine often overlooks. Foremost among their beliefs is in the sanctity of human life.</li> <li><i>The Grey Doves</i> are a rogue offshoot of the White Doves. Diplomats and missionaries, they believe their calling is to help end strife among the mundane and the Awakened. To this end, they work to improve inter-Tradition relations and atone for past mistakes. Members are famed as fair and honest arbitrators dedicated to fostering peace and understanding.</li> <li><i>The Children's Crusade</i> was founded in the 19th century to safeguard children and rescue them from abuse and exploitation. Members work around the globe to help child soldiers, orphans, runaways, slaves and victims of child trafficking.</li> </ul><br /> <b>The Anchorites</b> - Monastic ascetics and hermits seeking perfection and purity of the self while avoiding the sins of the modern world which distract from harmony with The One. While many of them try to avoid the Ascension War, it will often find them regardless.<ul><li><i>The White Robes</i> are a sect of Jain-influenced ascetics who have totally devoted their lives to non-harm to all living things. They are rarely found outside of India, where they are often protected by their less pacifistic Tradition-mates.</li> <li><i>The Children of Albi</i> originated among the medieval French Cathars and managed to survive that sect's destruction. Members reject modern society and technology, preferring to live in small isolated communes in Western Europe and the Americas. They are universally vegan and pacifist as they quietly pursue spiritual unity with The One.</li> <li><i>The Breath of Life</i> is a small movement of hermits who dwell alone in the wilderness. There they tend to the native wildlife and nature, seeking the Song of The One among the plants and animals.</li> <li><i>The Invisible Clergy</i> are radical ascetics who choose to dwell in abject poverty among society's lost and forgotten. There they serve as shepherds to those often seen as untouchables.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-19T10:18:26-07:00","id":1414581,"title":"","content":"Part of my headcanon is that the Traditional groups of Cabal's and Chantries are not mixed, and the PC's are unusual for working together across Traditions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-19T17:30:56-07:00","id":1414671,"title":"","content":"Personally, I don't think humans actually need avatars to Will-work. However, humans are really bad at it on their own.<br /> <br /> My reasons for this is that the magic of Every splat has some &quot;Willpower&quot; component(not the stat) that makes it work and the fact that humans/former-humans can Force an something to happen from willpower alone(spending willpower points).<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, humans are Really bad at both these things and either require a for of magic energy that's complimentary to the task(blood, gnosis, glamour, etc.) to work magic and willpower alone can't do much on it's own without magical assistance(you only get 1 success on spent willpower without using an obscure discipline/gift/etc. to increase that)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-10-31T19:46:55-07:00","id":1421253,"title":"","content":"Avatar Storm never happened.<br /> Control and Threat Null both exist as reflections of each other.<br /> Crossovers: Wraith and Changeling.<br /> The Traditions' Chantries were not all wiped out by the Technocracy.<br /> The Technocracy's Horizon Constructs are equally powerful.<br /> Umbral Lords are hospitable to the Traditions and see the presence of a Void Ship as an existential threat.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-10-31T20:23:56-07:00","id":1421254,"title":"","content":"So basically a direct projection of the early second edition setting, before the Chantries began to fall.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-11-01T18:56:38-08:00","id":1421375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1421254#post1421254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So basically a direct projection of the early second edition setting, before the Chantries began to fall.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. There are good reasons why I find this works. If you are storytelling a Traditions chronicle with newbie players, you want some default settings - preferences, let's say. But those settings have to be believable.<br /> <br /> 1. Zero point mentors provide some opportunities, because they must have been in the character's life at some point to anchor them in a Tradition. Mentor's are really useful for expository dialogue. It lets players determine the length and frequency of said info-dumps. The technique I use most often to balance this is: Mentor's interests sometimes directly conflict with a Seeking player's Avatar initiates. It's nice when you succeed at the Seeking and get some Library access before spending XP.<br /> <br /> 2. Players can earn admittance into a Chantry without precious background points spent in the beginning. This is balanced by the fact that the character will have to adhere to strict security protocols in their day to day life, if they join. Mind-blind Mages who would come and go, interacting with people in the Chantry, must consider enemy Mind Mages getting line of site and gleaning information by proxy-espionage. In a recent session, the cabal had made discoveries worthy of the Chantry's attention. When one player shared this with her mentor, she was informed of the existence of the Chantry. The player refused entry, for the time being, not wishing to adhere to such restrictions. That illustrates the balancing factor. They shared knowledge of the Chantry with the Cabal, initiating a debate that will be ongoing, driving roleplay.<br /> <br /> 3. The Technocracy is far more powerful than the Traditions on Earth; more Mages, more ECs and a Consensus that advantages them. The existence of a Chantry explains why the Union doesn't just steamroll the Trads out of their little Reality Zones (more on these below). You want both &quot;Traditions are underdogs.&quot; and &quot;Technocrats must consider plans against them carefully.&quot;. Not knowing everything about the Chantry explains the Technocracy's apprehension - and you know how they are about details. Otherwise, I don't see why they would not finalize their claim over the Earth.<br /> <br /> 4. I love the concept of Seasons. A Chantry in Winter is powerful but divided. A Chantry in Spring is resource-poor but united. Either way, or with the other two seasons, the players' actions determine how fast the season changes. They are totally permitted to consider just stealing everything they can get and skipping &quot;town&quot;, if they sufficiently detest a Chantry higherup. The Chantry is the place where an inhuman NPC can believably exist.<br /> <br /> 5. Chantry Mages have reasons to mislead the characters, given the security concerns already mentioned. This can produce conflict for candidates, possibly leading to rapid advancement, if they turn the deception against the liar. This is also a good way to familiarize the players with their sensory abilities instead of just skipping to more powerful Sphere-effects. A Cabal who choses independence over membership can be a client that relays information and poses little risk if they are captured.<br /> <br /> 6. Unaffiliated Trad Mages can forge relationships with low ranked Technomancers, under the right circumstances. The low ranked Technomancer will have colleges or superiors in the background whispering encouragement to develop the relationship to the Union's advantage.<br /> <br /> 7. Forcing the players to deal with a lot of problems in their Earthly neighborhood makes them appreciate the Horizon Realm when they earn access to it.<br /> <br /> To finish on Reality Zones on Earth, I'm quite subtle with these. I keep the laws of physics uniform across the Earth. Cellphone cameras' and the internet make widely varied physical laws on Earth implausible, in my view. The exception is where the RZ makes documentation impossible. If the Technocracy's plans conflict with the Cultural Values of locals, their Magick requires more effort, time and provide defending Trad players chances at sabotage, if they catch on quickly enough. Visibly reversing such attempts strengthens the players' standing in sleeper society and improves the benefits of the RZ. Even with Chantries as a fixture, my chronicles emphasize the importance of Sleepers, who are often the people most cherished by the players.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":26467,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","user":"HorizonParty2021","date":". 11-01-2020, 10:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-11-09T14:17:41-08:00","id":1422824,"title":"","content":"In my setting, you can travel between Classic WOD Earth and Chronicles of Darkness Earth. Watching Pentacle and Council Mages yell at each other is hilarious.<br /> <br /> Councilor - &quot;These dudes bleed magic! What do they call it, a nimbus? Crazy!&quot;<br /> Pentacle Mage - 'THEY HAVE INSIDE THEMSELVES A MINIATURE WATCHTOWER&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6358-robert-armsley","name":"Robert Armsley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6358_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}